nual report\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ROYALARC_09092025161845_CORRIGENDUM_TO_THE_ANNUAL_REPORT_FOR_FY_2024-2025.pdf\"","\"2025-09-09\"","\"65-102\"","\"4bee2e370752aec182c4d9211ad804b216c3a9751b0ed7da814bc222ef4b72ba\"","\"Final IPO prospectus\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Propsectus-Royal-Arc-18.02.2025.pdf\"","\"2025-02-18\"","\"32-60,143-162,201-239\"","\"aa08e0a3a0a7adb78e65deb91e48674653fc34b459edf0729625f4e623339a13\"","\"FY24 original annual report\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Annual-Report-2023-24.pdf\"","\"2024-07-03 audit\"","\"37-72\"","\"3e988eb0e955d9ed2217677e4220358cfe21b7f6ed36fcb084a0d0315f1ecd15\"","\"FY23 original annual report\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2022-23.pdf\"","\"2023 audit\"","\"2-35\"","\"28db60191b225030bd1dd51c1f77d7b18042fcf804855880d6b2d0fa63fa1ad3\"","\"FY22 original annual report\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2021-22.pdf\"","\"2022 audit\"","\"2-36\"","\"2a7d39b160291371f3b073c26c82b9fb3e138041eec373c9aa28e356eaa7c82f\"","\"FY21 original annual report\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2020-21.pdf\"","\"2021 audit\"","\"2-33\"","\"e3ec971ce179580bdf50cc71e0528667637f83a9b0ffb733d83003b9b5a8af41\"","\"S08\"","\"H1 FY26 results\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Outcome-of-BM-10-Nov-2025.pdf\"","\"2025-11-10\"","\"financial statements\"","\"e7fbae2c28f1eb640427c5d904f39c996e03b83a39000fd93efbb771e4a56d7d\"","\"S09\"","\"FY26 audited results\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ROYALARC_19052026171747_Outcome_of_BM_19052026.pdf\"","\"2026-05-19\"","\"all20pages\"","\"d9a0fc1bfd45217fefdc88641af47ffbb3087f1c5b296cd9425069ee67c8bb86\"","\"GEE FY26 annual report\"","\"https://www.geelimited.com/uploads/gee_reports/6c455225b15b0f70b908be16a04c7d6e.pdf\"","\"2026\"","\"76-80\"","\"a251ee4f06e0070c90c59d9c28143b7ed6013b13a841e1217b2165875f52de89\"","\"S11\"","\"ESAB FY26 annual report\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/chaithanya_lakshmi_esab_co_in_03072026114500_Notice_of_Shareholders_Meeting_2026_Signed.pdf\"","\"2026-07-03\"","\"106-108\"","\"07d62d6dac60820b5db38f98ab9520f7f51473e75802c7aa85e71890320552ff\"","\"S12\"","\"FY26 shareholding\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/SHP-31.03.2026-RAEL.html\"","\"alltables\"","\"a7a77b015c957624ce2577e58c449a8588b55bbdc304519a69ca100742e696fb\"","\"S13\"","\"FY19 original annual accounts\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2018-19.pdf\"","\"2019 original audited year\"","\"FY19 audit pp5-13; FY20 audit pp2-11 and financials pp12-14, note8p21\"","\"e101e991950a5b798e465dd6ad9feffcc57585fa5177056cd3eb3f80c8a33bf9\"","\"Historical primary document; raw tax appendices excluded from artifact\"","\"S14\"","\"FY20 original annual accounts\"","\"https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2019-20.pdf\"","\"2020 original audited year\"","\"a068ad121cc6b2ce44766d2cd7fa97ae7cc2237cb6acba1935923b87d019d1ce\"","\"Ador Welding FY26 revised audited standalone results\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ADORWELD_29042026203614_revisedAFR.pdf\"","\"2026-04-29\"","\"PDF pp5-10; P&L p5; segments p6; cashflow p8; normalization notes9-10\"","\"fb2bbd18f36a7076dc186f2ed764344c3ba4bc1b659771e295a38970dc1e275b\"","\"Pre-cutoff audited primary results\"","\"S16\"","\"ESAB July7 corrigendum and revised FY26 annual report\"","\"https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/chaithanya_lakshmi_esab_co_in_07072026121432_Correigendum_signed.pdf\"","\"2026-07-07\"","\"Cover and printed107-108 (PDF109-110)\"","\"205dd0da3c56f037dcafbadf35b46cacf9f981648bdeea738ec55e8d7b626158\"","\"Primary corrigendum says no financial-statement impact; P&L/cashflow numeric tokens matched July3 version. July29 revision subsequently identified but primary attachment returned403 and remains unverified.\"","\"# Royal Arc Electrodes Limited | SME research | v3.0\\n\\n## 1. Executive verdict\\n\\n**Hold, not an investable ranking.** Thesis archetype: industrial/cyclical consumables with a manufacturing and working-capital story, not a proven compounder. Permanent-loss risk is **High** under current evidence: potentially uncollectible assets, uncertain liability cash settlement, capital-intensive growth and difficult SME exit can impair per-share capital permanently; this is a risk assessment, not proof of imminent default. Royal Arc is a real welding-consumables manufacturer, not simply an order-book story. However, the evidence does not presently support a reliable organic-growth score or a favorable new-purchase decision. The decisive issues are the FY26 receivable note showing ₹2.9709 crore classified as doubtful with no allowance, the ₹4.2034 crore unexplained “other provisions,” and contradictory audit/notes wording. These are material reliability questions, not allegations of fraud. A good reported net-cash balance or a low-looking headline multiple does not resolve them. [S01, notes 9, 12, 15 and CARO]\\n\\nThe last observed exchange close was ₹164.50 on 16 October 2026, not a demonstrated executable 19 October quote. At 11,100,200 shares this implies ₹182.59829 crore equity value. The 1,200-share lot costs ₹197,400. A ₹5 lakh reference position rounds up to 3,600 shares costing ₹592,200; rounding down allows only 2 lots/₹394,800. Execution is a first-order constraint, not a footnote. [Official 60-session extracts and 19 October security master]\\n\\nThe favorable case requires repeat consumable demand, better receivables, genuine utilization improvements and a differentiated mix. The unfavorable case is nominal commodity-led sales expansion consuming cash while profit, export revenue and incremental capital productivity fail to follow. FY26 revenue grew 14.04%, but the revenue-minus-materials/inventory-change value-added proxy was essentially unchanged. No assumption that all sales growth is real volume growth is justified. [S01, S03]\\n\\n**Decision:** do not initiate solely from this analysis. Retain in Pending evidence, with G2 and G4 unresolved. This is neither a proven governance-failure designation nor a compounder classification. Overall conclusion confidence is **Low**, reflecting the weakest material evidence, not an average of strong source retrieval and weak economics.\\n\\n## 2. Research header, scope and gates\\n\\n- Issuer: Royal Arc Electrodes Limited; NSE Emerge symbol ROYALARC; ISIN INE0EO401019; CIN L31100MH1996PLC096296. Do not confuse it with similarly named infrastructure entities. [S01 cover; S03 corporate history]\\n- Information cutoff: 20 October 2026. Price/market cutoff: 19 October 2026. Retrieval occurred later, but sources admitted here were published by the cutoff. No post-cutoff operating results are backfilled.\\n- Financial scope: standalone Indian Accounting Standards under the Companies (Accounting Standards) Rules, 2021, **not Ind AS**. The FY26 directors' report's Ind AS wording conflicts with the auditor and accounting-policy basis. No consolidated subsidiary earnings are silently introduced. “Associate Entity” in the related-party list does not establish an equity-accounted associate. [S01 pp66,81 and RPT note]\\n- Financial anchor: audited year ended 31 March 2026. Latest interim before that is H1 FY26; it is not added to FY26 to invent a TTM period. No FY27 quarterly or half-year cash flow is estimated and presented as reported.\\n- Framework: recovered Small-Micro-Cap-Stock-Research-Master-Prompt-v3.0; five-year ownership horizon; 15% nominal INR return hurdle with 12%/18% alternatives; ₹5 lakh reference position; exact Decimal arithmetic.\\n- Sector rubric: industrial consumables manufacturing plus a smaller trading component. Organic units/value added, product qualification, customer concentration, inventory/receivable financing, maintenance/growth capex and incremental returns are the relevant tests. There is no sector-specific weight alteration.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Repeated historical statutory loan violations are evidenced. Year-end repayment and later compliance language mitigate, but FY25 loan-direction and control-resolution gaps remain. Present severe diversion/minority harm is not proven. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | ₹2.9709cr doubtful AR with nil allowance; ₹4.2034cr unexplained other provisions; contradictory CARO investment and contingency disclosures. Obtain ledger, collections, settlement and auditor reconciliations. |\\n| G3 | Conditional | Low audited debt, but March cash is not October cash, provisions and capex consume funds. Cash models are conditional and rescue is uncommitted; no verified current default. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Stale last observed trade and incomplete security-row coverage; observed-only ₹5lakh test is not executable and does not establish an actual 60-day exit. |\\n\\nA Conditional gate is not Clear. G3 is not marked Fail merely because a hypothetical scenario fails: current cash, obligations and committed facilities have not been verified at entry. Conversely, the scenario failure cannot be cured by assuming an unlimited capital raise. A severe evidenced present G1 failure would change the designation and trigger the framework's governance cap; that threshold has not been asserted here.\\n\\n## 3. Canonical scorecard\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence / evidence | Reason | Contrary fact |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium;E11,E12,E13,E15 | Three years of Section185 director-loan contraventions, repaid by FY23; later wording reports compliance but FY25 RPT loan direction is contradictory. Historical and current reliability issues require a hold, not an unproven diversion allegation. | Director loans were repaid by FY23 and AS15 was restated; lower remuneration and later compliance wording mitigate the historical findings. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium;E07,E08,E19,E23 | Qualified consumables and repeat orders, but no verified durable price premium, margin persistence, market share or switching-cost economics. | Approvals and repeat consumable demand plausibly create customer friction; no quantified premium or exclusivity was found. |\\n| REV | NS | Low;E02,E07,E09,E10 | Nominal audited growth is calculable but latest volume/price/mix bridge and commodity-adjusted economic growth cannot be established. | FY26 nominal revenue grew14.04%, but value added was nearly flat and current unit/price decomposition is missing. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium;E07,E10,E19 | Recurring consumables across fabrication/repair sectors, offset industrial cyclicality, top-ten concentration and export collapse. | Consumables recur across uses, but export contraction and concentrated customers can impair recurrence. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low;E08,E24,E25 | Broad incremental-return proxy deteriorates, but provision classification, doubtful AR, asset maturity and harmonized IC are material unresolved denominators; do not force a point score. | The Covid-base five-year proxy looks strong and CWIP may mature; neither resolves current asset/provision classifications or negative three-year incremental proxy. |\\n| CASH | 4 | Medium;E02,E04,E05,E14 | Audited ratio-of-sums plus persistent working-capital demands and recent negative FCF; FY26 provision-normalized conversion is worse, not a capex excuse for CFO. | Five-year reported CFO/PAT is0.77, and FY23 cash generation was strong; recent FCF and liability-supported FY26CFO are contrary evidence. |\\n| BAL | 6 | Medium;E05,E06,E14,E16,E20,E21 | Audited net cash and equity-funded expansion, subject to stale entry cash, claim settlement and intraperiod shortfall risks. | Low audited debt is real, but stale cash, opening liability settlements and uncertain facility availability limit its protection. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium;E08,E10,E11,E19 | Capacity/solar investment and lower remuneration are positives, but export contraction and lower incremental returns; utilization and commissioning outcomes unverified. | Expansion, solar and lower remuneration are positive execution evidence; measured utilization/payback and export continuity do not yet confirm it. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low;E02,E03,E04,E15 | Other income, allowance inconsistency, legacy restatement and unusual provisions prevent reliable organic normalized earnings-growth score. | Reported PAT rose in FY26; that does not resolve doubtful allowance or establish normalized recurring earnings growth. |\\n| R | NS | Low;E17,E20,E21,E22 | Conditional modeled returns cannot cure financial-reliability and entry-cash gaps. | Some bull paths survive without finance and reach break-even, but current evidence holds and hurdle tests prevent an investable point score. |\\n| D | 3 | Medium;E03,E04,E16,E18,E20 | Limited cash buffer under collection/capex stress, dilution and concentrated exit liquidity; not a price-derived favorable downside claim. | March net cash and repeat demand provide protection; they do not prevent collection/capex timing stress or SME exit constraints. |\\n| P | NS | Low;E17,E20,E22,E23 | No reliable conservative intrinsic-value margin of safety while G2 remains on hold. | Trailing18.18xPAT can look moderate; reliable normalized capital/cash and intrinsic-value support remain unavailable. |\\n| L | NS | Low;E18 | 21 missing security rows are not verified zero-volume days; no contemporaneous depth/circuit restriction evidence, and whole-lot participation fails observed-only diagnostics. | Observed trading and a19200-share latest session show some activity; missing sessions, lot size and absent live depth prevent executable liquidity scoring. |\\n\\n| Score | Raw/final | Coverage | Exact bound | Display bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | NS | 76.00% | [Decimal('3.54'), Decimal('5.70')] | ['3.5', '5.7'] |\\n| GQS | NS | 30.00% | [Decimal('2.10'), Decimal('8.40')] | ['2.1', '8.4'] |\\n| IAS | NS | 20.00% | [Decimal('1.40'), Decimal('8.60')] | ['1.4', '8.6'] |\\n\\nIndividual contributions below use the same canonical factors; NS is not a zero contribution. Evidence IDs resolve in evidence_ledger.json to public primary sources and precise locators.\\n\\n| Aggregate | Factor | Weight | Score | Weight × score |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | GOV | 0.20 | 4 | 0.8000 |\\n| BQS | MOAT | 0.20 | 4 | 0.8000 |\\n| BQS | REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.5000 |\\n| BQS | CAP | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | CASH | 0.10 | 4 | 0.4000 |\\n| BQS | BAL | 0.08 | 6 | 0.4800 |\\n| BQS | EXEC | 0.08 | 4 | 0.3200 |\\n| GQS | REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.0000 |\\n| GQS | EARN | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | CAP | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | CASH | 0.10 | 4 | 0.4000 |\\n| IAS | Q | 0.25 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | R | 0.35 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | D | 0.20 | 3 | 0.6000 |\\n| IAS | P | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | L | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n\\nThe 13 canonical factors are reused, not independently rescaled for different aggregate scores. BQS weights are 20/20/12/10/12/10/8/8; GQS 40/20/15/15/10; IAS 25/35/20/10/10. Each sums to 100%. IAS Q is the **unrounded final BQS**, which is NS here; it is not replaced with the midpoint of the BQS range. For any incomplete score, bounds are known weighted contribution plus missing weight ×1 and ×10. Bounds are uncertainty ranges, not forecasts or substitute scores.\\n\\nNo numerical override is applied: GOV is 4 rather than ≤3, G1 does not fail, G3 is Conditional, and CASH=4 does not invoke the persistent-poor-cash ≤3 cap. G2 and G4 nevertheless make IAS NS and prohibit favorable investment classification. Display rounding is half-up to one decimal; underlying arithmetic and any future classification use exact, unrounded values. The bound is not a confidence interval. Factor sensitivity: MOAT4→3/5 shifts the BQS bound by−/+0.20 to3.34–5.50/3.74–5.90; BAL6→5/7 shifts it by−/+0.08 to3.46–5.62/3.62–5.78. GQS is unchanged for these two factors. IAS would move by0.05/0.02 respectively only if Q were fully scoreable; here IAS remainsNS and the gates do not change. This tests judgment sensitivity without converting uncertainty into a neutral score.\\n\\nSubject confidence: identity and direct statement extraction High; historical comparable earnings Medium/Low after restatements and contradictory notes; organic growth Low; capital returns Low; cash/funding at entry Low; governance resolution Medium/Low; execution Low. Forecast uncertainty is separate from whether a document was accurately read.\\n\\n## 4. Business model and economic drivers\\n\\nRoyal Arc manufactures coated welding electrodes and flux-cored wire at Zaroli, Umbergaon, Valsad, Gujarat. Manufacturing entails wire preparation/drawing, powder/formulation mixing, extrusion or forming, baking, testing and packing. Inputs include steel wire/strip, stainless and nickel materials and ferro-alloy powders. Buyers use consumables in fabrication, repair and maintenance of structures, boilers, pressure vessels, pipes and industrial equipment. Quality failures can be costly, making product qualification and reliable supply valuable. This establishes a plausible commercial mechanism, not a proven price premium. [S03 pp143–151]\\n\\nThe published product catalogue is broader than actual in-house output. The prospectus says TIG/MIG production had stopped after FY21 because trading was commercially preferable. FY22–H1 FY25 capacity tables show nil TIG/MIG production, although machines could be reassigned. It would be wrong to treat catalogue breadth as evidence that all listed product categories were manufactured at scale in FY26. The latest comparable product-volume disclosure remains missing. [S03 pp145,153]\\n\\nFY24 pre-adjustment product sales comprised about 50.73% flux-cored wire, 39.73% electrode categories and 9.54% traded products. H1 FY25 manufacturing was 87.22% and trading 12.78%; TIG/MIG trading was 10.50%. These are product-sales denominators, not automatically the audited revenue denominator. The product table reconciles via freight, rate differences and discounts, with minor residual differences to the financial revenue note. [S03 pp145–147]\\n\\nCash is not earned when an order is mentioned. Materials and staff precede finished goods, shipment creates receivables, and collections may lag. Customer advances are modest relative to receivables. FY26 trade receivables of ₹25.75 crore, inventory ₹13.1826  crore and supplier advances ₹4.6877 crore substantially exceed trade payables ₹1.5274  crore and customer advances ₹0.9257 crore. This is a working-capital-intensive business even with little reported bank debt. [S01 notes7,14–17]\\n\\nRaw materials consumed represented 76.90%, 66.72%, 63.97% and 79.14% of revenue in FY22, FY23, FY24 and H1 FY25 respectively. Changes in product mix, input costs and inventory therefore matter enormously. No universal contractual pass-through formula or fixed pass-through lag has been demonstrated. Higher metal prices can increase invoice revenue and funding needs without increasing economic value added. [S03 p144]\\n\\n## 5. Competitive position and moat\\n\\n**Qualification mechanism:** approved grades and customer testing may reduce willingness to switch in critical applications. Evidence is management's ABS/IBR/BIS and quality-testing disclosures; durability depends on consistent quality and repeat approvals. Failure mode: an already qualified competitor offers equivalent performance with better service/credit. Certification is not evidence of exclusivity or market leadership. [S03 p143]\\n\\n**Formulation and service mechanism:** grade selection, customized formulations and application support may improve retention. Evidence is product breadth and in-house testing, but customer-level retention, price realization and failure-rate data are ND. Failure mode: commoditized grades, price competition and product substitution. No unsupported “top three” market-share claim is used.\\n\\n**Scale/distribution mechanism:** repeat dealer and industrial relationships can reduce customer acquisition effort, but Royal Arc lacks the verified scale and technology economics of a much larger peer such as ESAB. The latest concentration table has top-ten customers at 44.19% in FY24 and 47.84% in H1 FY25; top customer 8.75% and 10.65%. Diversified end-use descriptions do not eliminate revenue concentration. [S03 customer table pp157–159]\\n\\nA capable entrant needs formulation skills, quality systems, commercial relationships and working capital, not merely a basic production line. Yet there is no quantified entrant payback, switching-cost premium or durable excess-margin series. Hence MOAT=4. The thesis should be tested with repeat-customer volumes, customer churn, realized prices relative to input costs, rejection rates and contribution per tonne, not a list of certifications.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue growth and earnings quality\\n\\nAll figures below are ₹crore. FY19–FY21 are original audited AS reporting; FY22–24 use the prospectus restatement; FY25–26 use the latest audited statements. FY22–26 capex marked * is the published net cash purchase/sale line, not a fabricated gross purchase number; FY19–21 show gross purchases, with FY19/FY20 disposals separately retained. Operating EBIT excludes other income and adds finance costs; FY25's ₹0.4976 crore exceptional expense remains in the conservative reported operating bridge.\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | PAT | CFO | Cash capex* | FCF | Operating EBIT |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY19 | 75.79 | 1.26 | 5.22 | 0.29 | 4.93 | 3.35 |\\n| FY20 | 64.56 | 1.19 | 6.03 | 0.21 | 5.82 | 1.79 |\\n| FY21 | 43.32 | 1.57 | 7.32 | 2.37 | 4.94 | 1.06 |\\n| FY22 | 62.91 | 2.12 | 2.40 | 1.14 | 1.27 | 1.87 |\\n| FY23 | 96.94 | 9.57 | 12.59 | 1.17 | 11.41 | 12.57 |\\n| FY24 | 99.79 | 11.93 | 5.63 | 5.08 | 0.54 | 15.15 |\\n| FY25 | 101.77 | 9.01 | 4.83 | 7.76 | -2.93 | 11.68 |\\n| FY26 | 116.06 | 10.05 | 7.53 | 11.42 | -3.89 | 11.91 |\\n\\n| FY | Equity | Debt incl.current | Total cash | Fixed incl.CWIP | AR | Inventory | IC proxy | EPS end-share adjusted |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY19 | 16.10 | 20.37 | 2.45 | 16.35 | 14.09 | 14.88 | 34.00 | 1.35 |\\n| FY20 | 17.29 | 12.20 | 0.73 | 14.82 | 10.81 | 10.90 | 28.75 | 1.28 |\\n| FY21 | 18.87 | 8.23 | 0.30 | 15.51 | 10.65 | 8.74 | 26.77 | 1.69 |\\n| FY22 | 20.78 | 7.81 | 0.05 | 14.88 | 13.09 | 10.84 | 28.52 | 2.28 |\\n| FY23 | 30.35 | 1.42 | 3.02 | 13.96 | 16.44 | 6.07 | 27.39 | 10.29 |\\n| FY24 | 42.27 | 0.20 | 0.96 | 17.24 | 20.68 | 9.04 | 39.29 | 12.82 |\\n| FY25 | 71.78 | 0.81 | 19.30 | 22.05 | 20.93 | 14.28 | 51.81 | 8.11 |\\n| FY26 | 81.27 | 0.20 | 14.49 | 29.94 | 25.75 | 13.18 | 65.10 | 9.05 |\\n\\nCapital and per-share inputs are shown above; fully diluted issued shares equal the basic issued count at the dated no-convertible disclosure. End-share EPS is a comparable denominator diagnostic, not reported weighted-average EPS. The February 2024 bonus was 411 for every 100 shares, factor 5.11, not a cash-funded dilution; the February 2025 IPO fresh shares were dilution. Exact EBITDA, reported/normalized PAT, net debt, ROE, financing-capital ROIC, receivable/inventory/payable days and cash-cycle proxies are in financial_metrics.json. Inventory/payable days use material-plus-inventory-change cost as a disclosed proxy, not exact credit purchases or fully allocated manufacturing COGS. Original-period leases and contract assets were not separately disclosed; no material hidden lease is assumed away. No acquisition was identified in the reviewed standalone history. [S03 capital history; S01 financial notes]\\n\\nRevenue grew from ₹43.3228 crore in pandemic-affected FY21 to ₹116.06 crore in FY26. The five-year CAGR is mechanically high and base-distorted; it is not the preferred organic-growth anchor. The FY23–26 three-year revenue CAGR is much lower, about 6.2%. FY19/FY20 original revenue was ₹75.7875/₹64.5624 crore, reinforcing the FY21 base distortion and showing earlier contraction rather than a straight growth line. FY19/FY20 cash capex is gross and disposals are separately retained; original total debt includes current maturities in other current liabilities, not just borrowing headings. [S13–S14] The complete exact CAGR calculations and annual absolute additions are in the calculation artifact. A ten-year CAGR is unavailable because no verified eleven-observation comparable annual series was assembled; no nine- or eight-year series is relabelled ten years. A four-year restated series is not misrepresented as a five-year CAGR. [S01–S07]\\n\\nThe original FY22/FY23/FY24 revenue figures were ₹63.2553/₹97.3190/₹100.1073 crore versus restated ₹62.9097/₹96.9402/₹99.7875 crore. Original PAT was ₹2.1212/₹9.6955/₹12.1282 crore versus restated ₹2.1213/₹9.5682/₹11.9263 crore. Reclassifications, gratuity and other adjustments must remain visible. The prospectus restatement bridge contains its own confusing total; use the financial statements and retain the bridge, not a favorable unexplained splice. [S03 pp201–239; S04–S06]\\n\\n**Economic cross-check:** revenue less materials consumed and finished-goods/WIP inventory change, a value-added proxy rather than audited gross profit, was approximately ₹30.04 crore in FY23, ₹33.86 crore in FY24, ₹32.31 crore in FY25 and ₹32.34 crore in FY26. It excludes staff and other factory costs, so it must not be labelled true gross profit. FY26 nominal sales rose sharply without a corresponding value-added increase. No FY25/FY26 unit/price/mix bridge is disclosed in the sources reviewed; REV remains NS rather than applying the revenue-growth scoring ladder to nominal commodity invoices. [S01, S03]\\n\\nExports fell from ₹17.0083 crore in FY25 to ₹3.8904 crore in FY26, about 77.1%. Current broad claims about export opportunity and countries served must not override this realized contraction. Domestic growth may offset exports in the total, but profitability and customer quality of that replacement mix are unknown. [S01 note32]\\n\\nFY26 other income was ₹1.9435 crore, including ₹0.8735 crore interest and ₹0.9001 crore FX gains. Operating EBIT is ₹11.9127 crore versus reported PBT ₹13.618 crore, and normalized-tax operating NOPAT is ₹8.9345 crore before financing. Other income is not automatically recurring operating growth; FX treatment is disclosed rather than blindly called extraordinary. FY25 PAT of ₹9.0056 crore also bears the exceptional expense and a shorter weighted-average share denominator. FY26 reported EPS ₹9.05 versus FY25 ₹9.49 is not evidence of declining total PAT: IPO share timing changed weighted average shares. At current issued shares, FY25 PAT implies about ₹8.11/share. [S01 statements and notes19,29]\\n\\nH1 FY26 revenue ₹52.8390  crore and PAT ₹4.3146 crore compare with H1 FY25 restated ₹45.6047  crore and ₹3.1750 crore. They show improvement but are already included in FY26. There is no claim that these data constitute current FY27 momentum. [S08, S03]\\n\\n## 7. Growth runway and reinvestment economics\\n\\nThe prospectus's installed annual electrode capacity was 10,800 tonnes; FY24 production 3,459 tonnes, or 32.03%. Flux-cored-wire capacity increased to 7,200 tonnes, FY24 production 2,879 tonnes, or 39.99%. H1 FY25 production was 1,951 and 1,500 tonnes respectively; annualized utilization 36.13% and 41.67%. FY22/FY23 electrode production was 3,522/2,944 tonnes and flux-cored wire 1,477/2,624 tonnes. This is uneven volume history, not automatic uninterrupted compounding. [S03 p153]\\n\\nCrucially, the denominator assumed 24-hour production while actual operations generally used an eight-hour shift plus overtime, with quality/process constraints described by management. The apparent two-thirds spare capacity is therefore not entirely free capacity available at unchanged cost. Extra shifts need labor, process control, working capital and orders. Product-interchangeable lines cannot each be counted at full simultaneous capacity. FY26 commissioning, line-level uptime, output and saleable tonnage remain ND. [S03 pp152–154]\\n\\nFY26 PPE/intangibles/CWIP reached ₹29.9424 crore, including ₹5.4314 crore CWIP, from ₹22.0496 crore. Cash capex was ₹11.416 crore. The difference between cash capex less depreciation and the fixed-asset change requires the disclosed asset movement/disposal/noncash bridge; the model exposes the residual instead of pretending cash capex equals net asset additions. The plant/solar investment may improve future output and energy cost, but an installation announcement is not a measured payback. FY26 chairman wording refers to 950 “KWh” solar versus prior kWp references; no precise energy-saving return is inferred from inconsistent units. [S01 fixed-asset schedule and chairman statement]\\n\\nThe accounting financing-capital proxy is equity plus interest-bearing debt less total cash and investments. It rose from ₹27.3892 crore in FY23 to ₹65.0967 crore in FY26, while normalized-tax operating NOPAT fell from ₹9.4254 crore to ₹8.9345 crore. The observed three-year incremental proxy is approximately **−1.30%**. This is not a project IRR and not proof every recent machine destroys value. It includes CWIP and is affected by tax/provision financing, doubtful assets, restatements and timing. A clean organic committed-capital denominator is not currently established, so CAP remains NS. [S01, S03; calculation bridge]\\n\\nThe model retains two-, three- and four-year changes, finance-side reconciliation, cumulative capex less D&A, fixed-capital change and residual net operating-capital change. The five-year original-basis numerical proxy is also provided, using ₹26.7744 crore FY21 financing capital after adding current debt maturities. It is strongly positive because it begins with depressed Covid-era NOPAT, unlike the negative three-year proxy. It is **not a harmonized five-year organic project return**: the gratuity/restatement and operating-liability basis differ. CAP remains NS; the contrast is a base/timing warning, not a reason to choose the favorable window. A commissioned-only return would be falsely flattering if CWIP were simply removed without matching the associated earnings. Required evidence is commissioning dates, actual utilization, incremental gross contribution, maintenance capex and a reconciled operating balance sheet. Compare prospective post-tax returns with a reasoned 14–18% nominal INR small-company capital-cost range, not a single unexamined universal WACC.\\n\\n## 8. Governance, ownership and execution\\n\\nControl is concentrated in the Sanghvi promoter family: 8,099,178 shares or 72.96% at 31 March 2026, with 27.04% public. The same filing reports no promoter pledge/NDU/other encumbrance and no outstanding warrants, convertibles or ESOPs; 2,258,900 shares (20.35% of total) remain locked in. These are dated declarations, not current October assertions. The latest half-year ownership update was not located by the cutoff. [S12] The IPO had 18 lakh new shares and 12 lakh OFS shares at ₹120: company gross fresh cash ₹21.6 crore, promoter/OFS proceeds ₹14.4 crore. The ₹36 crore offer headline is not company funding. Net fresh cash was approximately ₹20.4611 crore after issue costs. Pre-IPO 9,300,200 shares plus 1,800,000 new shares reconcile to 11,100,200. No bonus share is treated as economic dilution. [S03; S01 share capital; official security master]\\n\\nFY26 RPT director/family remuneration plus rent was approximately ₹1.9359 crore, about 19.3% of PAT. Remuneration decreased from the prior year and a director salary reduction was disclosed, which is a favorable development, but related rents and appointments still warrant minority scrutiny. Reappointments approved during 2026 must not be mistaken for evidence that future operating targets will be met. [S01 RPT/remuneration; June board and AGM notices]\\n\\n**Original audit chronology must survive the IPO restatement.** FY21's emphasis wording explicitly says the gratuity liability was not ascertained/provided and the opinion was modified in that respect. The original FY19 and FY20 audits contain the same wording. FY20 reports Section185/186 compliance and no director loans; the repeated loan contravention is documented in FY21–23, not retroactively attributed to FY20. [S13 p7; S14 pp4,7] FY22/FY23 original records show further actuarial-policy versus actual-provision inconsistency; FY23 note37 says no present/future gratuity liability was ascertained or provided despite the accounting policy saying actuarial provision/funding. The prospectus explicitly retrospectively adjusts AS15 liabilities/expenses in FY22–24, including equity adjustments of ₹0.4063 crore in FY22 and ₹0.5858 crore in FY23. This is positive remediation. Its examiner relies on earlier audits and does not reissue them. Later restatement and booked gratuity do not make the original reports clean. The restatement bridge also has conflicting signs/totals, so the controlling restated statements are retained rather than inventing corrections. [S05–S07; original FY19 audit p7; S03 restatement]\\n\\nFY21 CARO reports director loans contrary to Section185 with maximum outstanding ₹3.2691624 crore. FY22 repeats the finding at ₹4.457956 crore. FY23 repeats it at ₹1.7163102  crore and explicitly says the loans were repaid by year-end. Repayment resolves that balance, not the three-year control history. FY24 reports Sections185/186 compliance. FY25 says no loans/investments in CARO, but the RPT note labels ₹4 lakh “Loan given during the year” to a director; its direction needs reconciliation with director borrowing movements. It is therefore too strong to declare there were definitely no later loans. [S07 p7; S06 p8; S05 p8; S04 p43; S02 pp70,97]\\n\\nFY22/FY23 bank quarterly AR/AP returns differed from books, with no quarterly stock valuation system. FY24 still shows differences and the same stock issue. FY25 CARO does **not** certify reconciliation: it says no sanctioned working-capital limits above ₹5 crore, while security/personal-guarantee notes reference larger amounts. FY26 CARO says facilities above ₹5  crore and returns agree. The changes may reflect different limits or reporting, but no full historical reconciliation is supplied. [S04 p42; S02 p69; S01 CARO]\\n\\nThe prior statutory auditor resigned in October 2024; successor CARO describes appointment and no outgoing concerns. This is not automatically misconduct, nor should the absence of concerns erase prior adverse findings. FY26 unmodified opinion and effective-IFC conclusion are positive but do not override contradictions in notes. FY21/FY22 IFC reporting exemption must not be rewritten as an affirmative clean IFC audit. [S02 p72; S06–S07; S01]\\n\\nDated delivery record, separating spending from economic delivery:\\n\\n| Dated statement / observable milestone | Expected evidence | Observed delivery by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n|18Feb2025 IPO objects: fund expansion and working capital [S03]|Use-of-proceeds and commissioning, then output/collection evidence|FY26 utilization reports₹4.8866cr expansion and₹14cr working capital; CWIP₹5.4314cr at31Mar2026 [S01]|Funding deployed, not demonstrated project payback; line-level commissioning/utilization ND.|\\n|FY25/FY26 solar investment commentary [S02/S01]|Installed nameplate units, generation, energy-cost saving and capex return|Earlier450kWp versus later950 “KWh” wording|Installation claimed; units and realized savings not reconciled. No quantified payback target verified.|\\n|FY26 export growth/market opportunity narrative [S01]|Exports, contribution margin and collections|FY26 exports₹3.8904cr versusFY25₹17.0083cr|Realized contraction contradicts a simple expansion interpretation; no numerical future export promise verified.|\\n|18Feb2025 restatement AS15 adjustment [S03]|Actuarial liabilities and expense recognized consistently|Retrospective adjustment plus later gratuity provisions|Positive accounting remediation, with reconciliation glitches and original modified opinions retained.|\\n|1Oct2025 remuneration reduction [S01]|Lower actual related director remuneration|FY26 director/family salaries declined; rent remained related|Favorable delivery but not proof of improved operating capital returns.|\\n\\nExecution assessment is mixed: expansion and solar investment, reduced remuneration and listed-company disclosure are positives; realized export contraction, lower incremental productivity and contradictory disclosure quality prevent a high EXEC score. Management statements about recurring customers or new opportunities remain M evidence until actual volume, price and cash outcomes verify them.\\n\\n## 9. Financial forensics, cash and funding\\n\\n**Receivables:** FY26 note15 lists ₹2.9709 crore as undisputed but considered doubtful, versus ₹1.3266 crore prior year, while the allowance is nil versus ₹0.0581 crore. The doubtful balance equals about 29.6% of FY26 PAT. It could reflect a labelling or accounting error, but neither possibility permits treating it as unquestionably good. Obtain age-wise customer ledgers, subsequent bank collections, credit notes and the auditor's signed reconciliation. A modest one-line ageing caveat is inadequate. [S01 p92]\\n\\n**Provisions:** other short provisions rose from ₹0.2303 crore to ₹4.2034 crore. Total short-provision growth contributed about ₹4.1466 crore to CFO. Reported CFO was ₹7.528 crore; removing only the increment in other provisions gives ₹3.5549 crore, about 35.4% of PAT. This is an analytical sensitivity, not a replacement audited CFO or proof the liability will all settle immediately. Nature, reversals, expense counterpart and settlement dates are ND. The cash model separately pays this opening liability, rather than including it in working capital and then paying it twice. [S01 notes9 and cash flow]\\n\\n**Cash conversion:** the five-year FY22–26 ratio of summed CFO to summed PAT is approximately 0.77, and the three-year ratio is approximately 0.58. Exact sums, rather than averages of annual ratios, are retained. Inventory release helped FY23 CFO; increasing receivables, advances and falling payables hurt later years. FY25/FY26 reported FCF was −₹2.9297/−₹3.8880 crore. Capex explains the gap from CFO to FCF, not weak CFO itself. Interest is financing-classified in the published cash flow; analytical FCFF is separately constructed. [S01–S03]\\n\\n**Cash classification:** FY26 ₹14.4901 crore total cash/bank includes ₹0.0465 crore margin money under lien, with the published cash-equivalent subtotal(A) ₹14.4435 crore used as potentially accessible at 31 March. Rounded line items and totals differ by₹0.01 lakh; the model uses that published subtotal rather than inventing precision from subtracting rounded totals. The ₹2.447 crore favorable debit cash-credit balance is cash, not borrowing. Gross debt is ₹0.2038 crore. A cash-credit label must not cause cash and debt to be counted simultaneously. Noncurrent deposits and non-operating investments are not silently made spendable cash. [S01 note16]\\n\\n**Investments:** note12 includes ₹1.4566 crore investment property, cooperative shares and ₹0.4061 crore newly acquired quoted Cochin/L&T shares, yet CARO says no investments were made. The property is non-operating; it is excluded from base EV cash deduction and has no forecast disposal proceeds. An alternative book-value deduction is disclosed only as a valuation sensitivity. The property must never be counted both in operating returns and again as excess cash. [S01 notes12 and CARO]\\n\\n**Claims:** FY26's nil contingency table coexists with an ongoing FY19–20 GST show-cause notice of ₹0.3346 crore, described as unascertained. FY24 had VAT ₹1.3025 crore, GST notices, unquantified income/local-body tax issues and a June2024 TDS amount. Later generic no-dispute/no-litigation paragraphs do not establish each claim's settlement. The base model includes only disclosed booked claims and separately tests cash stresses; unquantified legal exposure remains ND, not zero. [S04 note30; S01 note30]\\n\\n**IPO and asset funding:** FY25 financing cash flow included fresh equity, not organically generated cash. FY26 cash decreased ₹4.8131 crore, from ₹19.3032 crore to ₹14.4901 crore: CFO ₹7.528 crore, investing outflow ₹10.9249  crore and financing outflow ₹1.4162 crore reconcile. Investing includes capex, investments and interest; financing includes debt repayment, interest and ₹0.555 crore interim dividend. The FY26 final ₹0.50/share dividend has a record date before our entry and is separately modelled in the March-to-entry bridge. The interim already reflected in audited cash is not deducted again. [S01 statements; AGM]\\n\\nIPO utilization reported expansion ₹4.8866 crore, working capital ₹14  crore and GCP approximately ₹2.5852 crore, leaving ₹0.1282 crore against gross fresh proceeds. Gross-versus-net issue costs must reconcile before treating that residual as incremental free cash. The model starts from audited bank cash, not audited cash plus IPO proceeds again. Guarantees or historic sanction amounts do not establish available October bank headroom. A current issuer-specific rating rationale was not located in issuer/disclosed-source and public rating searches; no inferred external credit rating is assigned. [S01 IPO utilization]\\n\\n## 10. Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\\n\\nAt the reference price, equity value is ₹182.59829 crore. Headline trailing P/E is about 18.18×; price/book about 2.25×. Using March accessible cash and debt, EV before any property/investment monetization is ₹168.35859 crore, about 14.13× operating EBIT and 10.91× operating EBITDA. These are cross-date diagnostics, not current executable fair value. Replacing operating earnings with total income or netting pledged cash would make them misleadingly attractive. [S01 and official market extracts]\\n\\n**SME market-maker overlay:** The prospectus names Shreni Shares Limited under the5December2024 market-making agreement, with153,600 offer shares reserved and a stated three-year commitment from listing. The March2026 shareholding filing shows Shreni holding630,000 shares,5.68% of total. This concentration and contractual role do not prove current two-way quote depth or guarantee an exit; current market-maker inventory and actual firm bid/ask quotes are ND. [S03 market-making section; S12]\\n\\n**Execution diagnostic:** 60 scheduled sessions from 24 July through 19 October contain 39 Royal rows and 21 absent security rows; 14 of 39 observed sessions have a single traded price. Observed-only median turnover is ₹5.46 lakh and mean about ₹13.06 lakh. At 2%/5%/10% of the lower observed-only statistic, the rounded-up position needs 54.23/21.69/10.85 fractional-session equivalents, but each participation allowance buys less than one 1,200-share lot. Thus a strict daily whole-lot implementation has zero permitted lots at all three rates. Missing rows are not proven zero-volume sessions, and one-price sessions alone do not prove circuit-locks. The master price band/status fields are retained, not decoded into an unsupported current restriction assurance. The genuine 60-session exit estimate and L remain NS. Recent20-session evidence is similarly thin:12 observed and8 absent rows, observed mean₹974,220, median₹450,930 and4 one-price sessions. The recent observed-only median is lower, not an improving-depth signal.\\n\\n**Model specification:** seven unequal April–19 October pre-entry periods are cash-linked to the audited March balance sheet, then sixty monthly ownership periods. No current cash balance is asserted. Each month has capex, opening claim settlements, finance costs, four supplier-before-customer collection cycles and tax. Supplier prepayment is 70% of modeled operating cash cost per cycle; 1/2/4/8-cycle sensitivities show the dependence on collection timing. A ₹1 crore minimum cash buffer is required at every event. Negative receipts due to collection/asset loss assumptions are not artificially floored away.\\n\\nYear-one sales use FY26 revenue ×(1+growth), not a fabricated interim report. Bear/base/bull sales growth is −5%/8%/15%; EBITDA margins 10%/13.30%/16%; DSO 120/90/75; maintenance capex 3.5% and D&A 3% of sales; fixed-capital target 30% of revenue. Working-capital targets explicitly include receivables, inventory, supplier advances, other assets, trade payables, customer advances and other operating liabilities. Growth capex is the extra capital needed after maintenance less D&A, so an extra maintenance amount is not counted again as growth capacity.\\n\\nPre-entry annual sales paces are ₹105/122/130  crore and capex ₹8/6/5 crore. These are analyst stress assumptions, not reported FY27 guidance. Doubtful-receivable losses are full/half/nil. Opening other provisions are settled over four months in the principal cases; settlement of zero/half/all is separately tested. Loss reduces collections consistently with the receivable bridge; it is not both an extra cash payment and a collection deduction. No tax benefit on the doubtful loss is assumed.\\n\\nExisting party debt is paid in the first pre-entry month, a stated conservative timing assumption. Opening long-term employee provisions ₹0.6940 crore and long-term deposits ₹0.2371 crore are conservatively settled in the first ownership month; they are outside ONWC and are not allowed to vanish at terminal. No cash timing for deferred tax is fabricated beyond normalized ongoing cash taxation. The optional financing case allows at most ₹5 crore uncommitted bank borrowing at 13%, then post-entry equity at ₹100/share (₹60 in the bear) with 5% fees, maximum ₹10 crore gross and the 18,000,000 authorized-share ceiling. Shares are whole numbers rounded upward to cover net proceeds. No pre-entry issue is permitted because the observed entry share count is fixed. Optional financing is sought **before** a payment would breach cash; if a shortfall still occurs, failure is irreversible. Subsequent rows continue only to expose the complete diagnostic economic path, never to restore an ordinary return.\\n\\n| Case / funding | Entry cash | Cash survives | First breach period | Optional bank | New shares | Exit price | Net CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear / no_new_finance | -10.63 | False | 3 | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS% |\\n| bear / bounded_optional | -5.79 | False | 5 | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS% |\\n| base / no_new_finance | 2.60 | False | 8 | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS% |\\n| base / bounded_optional | 2.60 | True | None | 1.56 | 0 | 82.50 | -13.59% |\\n| bull / no_new_finance | 13.16 | True | None | 0.00 | 0 | 187.30 | 1.81% |\\n| bull / bounded_optional | 13.16 | True | None | 0.00 | 0 | 187.30 | 1.81% |\\n\\nThe no-new-finance base can have positive year-end cash yet fail in the first ownership month's supplier-before-collection cycle. This is exactly why annual-only financing checks are insufficient. Optional base liquidity is conditional on a bank advance, not an established available facility. The bear fails even with the bounded bank case before entry; no later paper equity value is presented as a realizable return.\\n\\n**Earned terminal economics:** terminal value is not a chosen promotional P/E. Next-year NOPAT plus D&A, less maintenance capex, capacity growth, working-capital growth and required-buffer growth produces terminal FCFF. Discount rates are 18%/16%/14%, long-run growth 2%/4%/5% in bear/base/bull. The code rejects discount≤growth. A twelve-month terminal cash schedule settles all debt once, tests supplier/collection timing with no new finance and solves the retained operating reserve including its own growth. Equity value is EV plus cash less debt less that reserve. This is one debt claim, not duplicate terminal repayments; restricted cash and property are not added again. Ordinary return is NS if pre-entry, intraperiod or terminal survival fails.\\n\\nThe model reports terminal implied EV/EBIT and ROIC. Separate 8/10/12× EBIT cross-checks are diagnostics, not permission to substitute an unearned higher terminal multiple. The terminal asset target accounts for maintenance less D&A before requiring incremental fixed capital, consistent with explicit-period reinvestment. A high growth assumption that requires unfinanceable capital is rejected rather than rewarded with a large exit value.\\n\\nAll returns use diluted terminal shares, 2% entry costs and 2% exit costs, and no invented post-entry dividend distribution. Cash retained belongs to terminal shareholders; actual pre-entry dividends do not accrue to a new buyer. Transaction-cost assumptions do not solve whole-lot/depth constraints. Explicit zero/2%/5% friction sensitivities are stored for every surviving path; they are not assumed broker fee quotations.\\n\\nEach path also stores required gross exit price and terminal EV/EBIT to reach 12%/15%/18% after costs. These hurdle arithmetic checks remain labelled unattainable where the cash path fails, rather than implying that a richer multiple funds an earlier cash shortfall.\\n\\n**Reverse expectations:** growth is scanned from 0% through 40% in 1 percentage-point steps for base/bull economics and both financing modes, including the literal 0% price-implied break-even return and 12%/15%/18% hurdles, then feasible sign-changing hurdle brackets are bisected 48 times. A bracket cannot jump across a failed path. Every evaluated path, including failures and root intermediates, is retained and losslessly deduplicated by full input specification. “No root in this domain” does not prove no mathematical root exists elsewhere.\\n\\n| Case | Funding | Hurdle | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 0% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 0% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 0% | 10.2798% to 10.2798% |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 0% | 10.2798% to 10.2798% |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n\\nThere are 366 unique full cash paths in the canonical archive. The two-way growth/margin grid, discount/terminal-growth grid, DSO, opening capex, liability settlement, borrowing cap, issue-price, maintenance and collection-cycle sensitivities are recoverable from the compact inputs and code. Financial reliability, rather than a chosen root, remains the binding gate.\\n\\n**Primary economic peers:** these are economic comparisons, not same-date price-multiple anchors.\\n\\n| Company | FY26 revenue | Normalized EBIT margin | CFO/PAT | FCF |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| ESAB India | 1508.15 | 16.76% | 0.87 | 149.14 |\\n| GEE Limited | 369.1388 | 8.01% | -0.60 | -20.62 |\\n| Ador Welding | 1135.45 | 9.00% | 1.41 | 94.57 |\\n\\nESAB is much larger, with equipment, technology, group relationships and distribution advantages. Source-vintage limitation: the3July original was reconciled to the7July primary corrigendum/revised report, whose cover says no financial-statement impact and whose P&L/cash-flow numeric tokens match. A later29July revision was identified, but its primary attachment returned403; its full changes were not independently inspected. No claim of latest-version peer-audit review is made. [S11/S16] Its FY26 exceptional net gain ₹17.26 crore includes land gain ₹30.91 crore less labour-code expense ₹13.65 crore; the EBIT comparison removes the net exceptional gain and other income. GEE's rebound follows a FY25 loss, with negative FY26 CFO, asset-sale proceeds ₹30.442  crore and warrant cash ₹10.20 crore. It illustrates capital/cash risk, not a clean superior quality benchmark. Royal's EBIT margin lies between these businesses, but its receivable/provision reliability is unresolved. No peer's multiple is borrowed as a Royal target without normalized mix, cash and same-date primary price evidence. [S10 pp76–80; S11 pp106–108,145]\\n\\nAdor is the third verified economic peer, using its official revised audited FY26 results rather than the annual PDF whose retrieval timed out. Its mechanical pre-exception operating EBIT excludes other income but retains ₹24.82 crore onerous/LD costs and ₹14.12 crore historic Kuwait receivable recovery. Removing both would produce a different hypothetical margin; neither is silently normalized away. Welding segment revenue/result were ₹1,078.88/₹164.30crore, while flares/process were ₹56.71/−₹23.06crore. CFO benefited from ₹84.49 crore payables growth and the recovery. Prior merger and current subsidiary impairment also complicate trend comparisons. [S15 pp5–10]\\n\\n## 11. Strongest bear case and failure path\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is not merely a lower market multiple. It is that FY26 growth represents lower-value-added domestic invoices replacing exports, while cash realization is flattered by liability creation and doubtful receivables. Capital is already committed to fixed assets, inventory and supplier advances. A large customer pays late, provisions settle and expansion consumes cash before collections arrive. The reported March net-cash label then disappears at an intraperiod trough; a new buyer cannot assume later annual profits will bridge it.\\n\\nConcentration matters: the latest disclosed top-ten share approaches half sales, while current customer-level profitability is ND. Input-price increases can require additional working capital before customer repricing; low-quality receivables can absorb PAT. A continued shortage can force expensive debt or equity below the purchase price. SME trading lots and one-price sessions can prevent an exit precisely when the thesis fails.\\n\\nThe thesis-break variable is **cash collectible from the disputed/doubtful receivable population and the settlement nature/timing of other provisions**, followed by incremental contribution per unit of capital. A reconciled auditor explanation could improve reliability; continued unexplained contradictions, repeat director advances contrary to law or refusal to quantify cash obligations would deepen the gate problem. No specific wrongdoing is assumed without evidence.\\n\\n## 12. Multibagger and compounder test\\n\\nA doubling over five years requires about 14.87% annualized gross return, already close to the 15% hurdle before costs. Tripling requires about 24.57%. This cannot be justified by revenue CAGR alone. The model demands per-share value creation after reinvestment, operating reserves, debt and any new shares. Earnings funded by recurring equity issuance do not compound for the original shareholder at the company growth rate.\\n\\nThe favorable operating ingredients are repeat consumable demand, qualified products, a manufacturing base and potentially better utilization. The necessary evidence is missing: current real volumes/price realization, stable normalized margins, reliable collectible receivables, a reconciled provision book and attractive mature incremental returns. Apparent spare 24-hour capacity and a large addressable infrastructure market do not fill those gaps.\\n\\nThere is no compounder designation under v3.0. BQS/GQS are incomplete, governance/control history remains relevant, financial reliability is on hold and executable liquidity has not been established. The model may generate conditional high-growth paths, but those are boundary tests, not a probability-weighted multibagger claim.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring plan and explicit resolution tests\\n\\n1. Obtain the signed reconciliation of FY26 note15 doubtful classification and nil allowance, plus subsequent collections. Upgrade G2 only if the material balance and earnings effect reconcile, not because one total matches.\\n2. Identify each component of ₹4.2034 crore other provisions, expense counterpart, reversal/settlement, cash dates and related parties. Re-run entry cash and all intraperiod paths, not just terminal PAT.\\n3. Reconcile CARO no-investment wording with the securities purchases, FY25 RPT loan direction, and all open tax notices. Preserve prior audit findings rather than deleting them from the update history.\\n4. Obtain FY27 interim results and cash flow when publicly released; reconstruct March-to-entry cash using actual data and compare to scenario assumptions. This report does not claim an already scheduled monitoring service.\\n5. Track tonnes by product, manufacturing versus trading, realizations, material-cost lag, contribution per tonne, saleable capacity, shifts, utilization and commissioning dates. Only then consider REV/CAP/EARN point scores.\\n6. Verify committed available bank limits, covenants, security and debt schedules. Historic personal guarantees are not available cash.\\n7. Track customer concentration, receivable ageing, supplier advances, inventory days and payables. Three consecutive credible reporting periods with improved collections would be stronger than a one-off CFO spike.\\n8. Obtain all 60-session security-status/zero-trade evidence and live permitted-order depth before any execution conclusion. Respect actual lot size and market restrictions; do not assume fractional-lot liquidity.\\n9. Recompute valuation from a contemporaneous price and cash anchor. A lower price can improve IAS inputs only after gates permit scoring; it cannot make unreliable accounts reliable.\\n\\nMonitoring thresholds below are analyst decision rules, not company guidance, covenants or automatic factor caps. “Next filing” means the next publicly available statutory interim/annual record, not an assumed release date.\\n\\n| Item and dated baseline | Expected evidence / milestone | Warning threshold | Thesis-break / gate consequence |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n|31Mar2026 doubtfulAR₹2.9709cr, allowance nil [S01]|Signed auditor reconciliation and customer-wise subsequent collections at next filing|Material balance still unexplained or collection evidence absent|Proven large unprovided loss plus inability to fund obligations; escalateG2 and evaluateG3, never dismiss as rounding.|\\n|31Mar2026 other provisions₹4.2034cr [S01]|Nature, counterpart expense, related party and dated cash settlement schedule|Another material unexplained increase/reversal or missing schedule|Evidence that cash/earnings were materially misstated or obligations cannot be paid; retain/escalate reliability hold.|\\n|31Mar2026 AR₹25.75cr and supplier advances₹4.6877cr [S01]|H1FY27 cash conversion and bank collection bridge|DSO above90 days for two comparable reporting observations, or advances rise without deliveries|DSO approaches120 days with the model's unfunded cash trough; financing resilience thesis breaks unless committed cash is documented.|\\n|FY26 value added₹32.34cr, nearly flat [S01]|Product tonnes, realization, material lag and contribution per tonne|Sales grow but value added/collections again fail to grow|Sustained capital growth without corresponding operatingNOPAT/FCF invalidates the reinvestment thesis.|\\n|31Mar2026 CWIP₹5.4314cr [S01]|Commissioning dates, saleable output and incremental margins|Further capex before prior capacity's economics are disclosed|Repeated delay/cost growth plus unfunded capex or persistent poor mature returns.|\\n|FY26 exports₹3.8904cr [S01]|Geography/customer mix, export collections and contribution|Further decline without profitable cash-generating domestic replacement|Loss of key customers undermines the base demand/margin path; reassessDUR/EXEC rather than using country-count claims.|\\n|31Mar2026 accessible cash subtotal₹14.4435cr; debt₹0.2038cr [S01]|Actual interim cash plus committed facility and covenant evidence|Cash falls below modeled₹1cr buffer at any payment date|Verified unfinanceable essential obligation is G3Fail; an uncommitted issue is not a cure.|\\n|FY21–23 director-loan violations, FY25 direction discrepancy [S02/S05–07]|Signed RPT direction, repayment and statutory resolution|Fresh unexplained related advances or unresolved contradictory disclosure|Substantiated severe diversion/repeated minority harm triggersG1Fail/GOVoverride, not merely an auditor change.|\\n|19Oct market window39/60observed, lot1200 [market extract]|Complete zero-trade/status evidence and contemporaneous permitted-order depth|Daily5% participation remains below one whole lot or missing sessions persist|No reliable executable exit for the intended size: G4Hold/new-purchaseIASNS remains.|\\n\\nUpdate log: 20 October 2026 cutoff, first v3.0 research edition. Primary original-audit findings and official revised FY26 report supersede an initially discovered mirror; no earlier point score is silently revised. The official 104-page annual report matches the revised AGM attachment after its cover; the older mirror omitted an EPS/dividend accounting-policy paragraph but did not change the financial figures. Any future score change must distinguish new economics, evidence resolution and price effects.\\n\\n## 14. Classification and final quality checks\\n\\n**Classification: Hold — unresolved financial reliability and tradability. Pending evidence; excluded from investable ranking; no compounder or attractive-entry label.** Do not rank NS below scored companies as if it were a proven low-quality result. Do not compare this score arithmetically with older framework versions. No overall SME winner or portfolio exit count follows from a single-company report.\\n\\nThe following 16 checks are delivery checks, not claims that every missing item has been resolved:\\n\\n| # | Required check | Result |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Correct issuer/security/scope and explicit dates | Verified identity; standalone; stale quote explicitly dated. |\\n| 2 | Primary records; management/analyst attribution | Primary audit/results/prospectus/peer/market sources; forecast assumptions labelled. |\\n| 3 | Audit/RPT/funding/dilution and proportionate gates | Original adverse history retained; no unsupported fraud allegation; G2/G4 holds. |\\n| 4 | Comparable units/periods; no false TTM or quarterly CFO | ₹crore; restatement bridges; H1 not added to FY; FY27 cash ND. |\\n| 5 | Revenue versus profits/acquisitions/pass-through/base | Real-volume bridge ND and REV NS; Covid anchor and value-added cross-check explicit. |\\n| 6 | Ratio-of-sums CFO/PAT; capex distinction | Exact 3/5-year sums; separate CFO versus FCF and provision sensitivity. |\\n| 7 | Incremental return numerator/denominator/timing | Broad 2/3/4-year proxy with financing/asset residuals; five-year original-basis diagnostic separately flagged as non-harmonized. |\\n| 8 | Normalization, tax, claims, leases, dilution | Other-income/exception treatment; booked/unknown claims separated; no disclosed material lease liability assumed zero without noting source scope. |\\n| 9 | Linked scenarios and funding, no double counting | Pre-entry/monthly/intraperiod/terminal cash; whole-share limits; immutable failure; one debt deduction. |\\n| 10 | Canonical factors and correct weights | 13 factors, 4 gates, each score weights100%; Q uses unrounded final BQS. |\\n| 11 | Missingness/bounds/confidence | NS retained, no reweight, exact bounds; Low overall material confidence. |\\n| 12 | Raw/final/caps | All computable pieces shown; no unsupported cap; G2/G4 force no favorable IAS. |\\n| 13 | Matrix and compounder rules | Incomplete/hold classification, no invented quality band. |\\n| 14 | Stated position liquidity and stale quote | ₹5lakh,1200-share lot,2/5/10% participation diagnostics; actual execution NS. |\\n| 15 | Strongest bear and thesis-break | Collectibility/provision settlement/capital absorption explicitly linked. |\\n| 16 | Common methodology and incomplete status | v3.0 provenance retained; no ranking or batch-completion claim. |\\n\\n**Bottom line:** Royal Arc has an established operating business. What is not established is that recent revenue growth translates into collectible, capital-efficient per-share value at an executable price. Resolving that distinction is more important than squeezing a point target from uncertain accounts.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, evidence and reproduction\\n\\n- **S01** [FY26 audited annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Annual_Report_RAEL_FY_2025-2026.pdf), 2026-08, pages/sections 66-104.\\n- **S02** [FY25 corrected annual report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ROYALARC_09092025161845_CORRIGENDUM_TO_THE_ANNUAL_REPORT_FOR_FY_2024-2025.pdf), 2025-09-09, pages/sections 65-102.\\n- **S03** [Final IPO prospectus](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Propsectus-Royal-Arc-18.02.2025.pdf), 2025-02-18, pages/sections 32-60,143-162,201-239.\\n- **S04** [FY24 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Annual-Report-2023-24.pdf), 2024-07-03 audit, pages/sections 37-72.\\n- **S05** [FY23 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2022-23.pdf), 2023 audit, pages/sections 2-35.\\n- **S06** [FY22 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2021-22.pdf), 2022 audit, pages/sections 2-36.\\n- **S07** [FY21 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2020-21.pdf), 2021 audit, pages/sections 2-33.\\n- **S08** [H1 FY26 results](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Outcome-of-BM-10-Nov-2025.pdf), 2025-11-10, pages/sections financial statements.\\n- **S09** [FY26 audited results](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ROYALARC_19052026171747_Outcome_of_BM_19052026.pdf), 2026-05-19, pages/sections all20pages.\\n- **S10** [GEE FY26 annual report](https://www.geelimited.com/uploads/gee_reports/6c455225b15b0f70b908be16a04c7d6e.pdf), 2026, pages/sections 76-80.\\n- **S11** [ESAB FY26 annual report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/chaithanya_lakshmi_esab_co_in_03072026114500_Notice_of_Shareholders_Meeting_2026_Signed.pdf), 2026-07-03, pages/sections 106-108.\\n- **S12** [FY26 shareholding](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/SHP-31.03.2026-RAEL.html), 2026-03-31, pages/sections alltables.\\n- **S13** [FY19 original annual accounts](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2018-19.pdf), 2019 original audited year, pages/sections FY19 audit pp5-13; FY20 audit pp2-11 and financials pp12-14, note8p21.\\n- **S14** [FY20 original annual accounts](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2019-20.pdf), 2020 original audited year, pages/sections FY19 audit pp5-13; FY20 audit pp2-11 and financials pp12-14, note8p21.\\n- **S15** [Ador Welding FY26 revised audited standalone results](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ADORWELD_29042026203614_revisedAFR.pdf), 2026-04-29, pages/sections PDF pp5-10; P&L p5; segments p6; cashflow p8; normalization notes9-10.\\n- **S16** [ESAB July7 corrigendum and revised FY26 annual report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/chaithanya_lakshmi_esab_co_in_07072026121432_Correigendum_signed.pdf), 2026-07-07, pages/sections Cover and printed107-108 (PDF109-110).\\n\\nMarket source family: [NSE 19 October 2026 security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) and [19 October bhavcopy](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). The authored market extract retains all 60 exact daily URLs, ZIP/CSV hashes and matching rows; absent rows remain explicitly absent. Latest actual Royal observation is 16 October, not the master file's date. These are official sources, not evidence of currently available bid depth.\\n\\nSource register gives document identifiers, public URLs, dates, page locators and hashes. Evidence ledger distinguishes V verified disclosure, C calculation, M management statement and A analyst assumption; contradictions and confidence are explicit. Reproduction requires only the declared compact JSON/Markdown inputs and standard-library Python code. The full archive additionally contains generated canonical outputs, complete compressed path events and audit tables. Neither package includes raw PDFs, OCR, screenshots, download helpers, private identifiers, authentication material or external file-service identities.\\n\\nRun `python build.py`, then `python verify.py`. In a freshly extracted recovery copy run `python guarded_rebuild.py --clean`: generated outputs and old attestations are removed, undeclared data reads/network/subprocess calls are blocked during rebuilding, then hashes and arithmetic are independently checked. Packaging/recovery attestations describe actual tests; their presence alone is not evidence that a reconstruction succeeded. No external account action or trade is implied by these research artifacts.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive verdict\"","\"\\n**Hold, not an investable ranking.** Thesis archetype: industrial/cyclical consumables with a manufacturing and working-capital story, not a proven compounder. Permanent-loss risk is **High** under current evidence: potentially uncollectible assets, uncertain liability cash settlement, capital-intensive growth and difficult SME exit can impair per-share capital permanently; this is a risk assessment, not proof of imminent default. Royal Arc is a real welding-consumables manufacturer, not simply an order-book story. However, the evidence does not presently support a reliable organic-growth score or a favorable new-purchase decision. The decisive issues are the FY26 receivable note showing ₹2.9709 crore classified as doubtful with no allowance, the ₹4.2034 crore unexplained “other provisions,” and contradictory audit/notes wording. These are material reliability questions, not allegations of fraud. A good reported net-cash balance or a low-looking headline multiple does not resolve them. [S01, notes 9, 12, 15 and CARO]\\n\\nThe last observed exchange close was ₹164.50 on 16 October 2026, not a demonstrated executable 19 October quote. At 11,100,200 shares this implies ₹182.59829 crore equity value. The 1,200-share lot costs ₹197,400. A ₹5 lakh reference position rounds up to 3,600 shares costing ₹592,200; rounding down allows only 2 lots/₹394,800. Execution is a first-order constraint, not a footnote. [Official 60-session extracts and 19 October security master]\\n\\nThe favorable case requires repeat consumable demand, better receivables, genuine utilization improvements and a differentiated mix. The unfavorable case is nominal commodity-led sales expansion consuming cash while profit, export revenue and incremental capital productivity fail to follow. FY26 revenue grew 14.04%, but the revenue-minus-materials/inventory-change value-added proxy was essentially unchanged. No assumption that all sales growth is real volume growth is justified. [S01, S03]\\n\\n**Decision:** do not initiate solely from this analysis. Retain in Pending evidence, with G2 and G4 unresolved. This is neither a proven governance-failure designation nor a compounder classification. Overall conclusion confidence is **Low**, reflecting the weakest material evidence, not an average of strong source retrieval and weak economics.\\n\\n\"","\"Research header, scope and gates\"","\"\\n- Issuer: Royal Arc Electrodes Limited; NSE Emerge symbol ROYALARC; ISIN INE0EO401019; CIN L31100MH1996PLC096296. Do not confuse it with similarly named infrastructure entities. [S01 cover; S03 corporate history]\\n- Information cutoff: 20 October 2026. Price/market cutoff: 19 October 2026. Retrieval occurred later, but sources admitted here were published by the cutoff. No post-cutoff operating results are backfilled.\\n- Financial scope: standalone Indian Accounting Standards under the Companies (Accounting Standards) Rules, 2021, **not Ind AS**. The FY26 directors' report's Ind AS wording conflicts with the auditor and accounting-policy basis. No consolidated subsidiary earnings are silently introduced. “Associate Entity” in the related-party list does not establish an equity-accounted associate. [S01 pp66,81 and RPT note]\\n- Financial anchor: audited year ended 31 March 2026. Latest interim before that is H1 FY26; it is not added to FY26 to invent a TTM period. No FY27 quarterly or half-year cash flow is estimated and presented as reported.\\n- Framework: recovered Small-Micro-Cap-Stock-Research-Master-Prompt-v3.0; five-year ownership horizon; 15% nominal INR return hurdle with 12%/18% alternatives; ₹5 lakh reference position; exact Decimal arithmetic.\\n- Sector rubric: industrial consumables manufacturing plus a smaller trading component. Organic units/value added, product qualification, customer concentration, inventory/receivable financing, maintenance/growth capex and incremental returns are the relevant tests. There is no sector-specific weight alteration.\\n\\n| Gate | Status | Reason |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| G1 | Conditional | Repeated historical statutory loan violations are evidenced. Year-end repayment and later compliance language mitigate, but FY25 loan-direction and control-resolution gaps remain. Present severe diversion/minority harm is not proven. |\\n| G2 | Hold — unresolved | ₹2.9709cr doubtful AR with nil allowance; ₹4.2034cr unexplained other provisions; contradictory CARO investment and contingency disclosures. Obtain ledger, collections, settlement and auditor reconciliations. |\\n| G3 | Conditional | Low audited debt, but March cash is not October cash, provisions and capex consume funds. Cash models are conditional and rescue is uncommitted; no verified current default. |\\n| G4 | Hold — unresolved | Stale last observed trade and incomplete security-row coverage; observed-only ₹5lakh test is not executable and does not establish an actual 60-day exit. |\\n\\nA Conditional gate is not Clear. G3 is not marked Fail merely because a hypothetical scenario fails: current cash, obligations and committed facilities have not been verified at entry. Conversely, the scenario failure cannot be cured by assuming an unlimited capital raise. A severe evidenced present G1 failure would change the designation and trigger the framework's governance cap; that threshold has not been asserted here.\\n\\n\"","\"Canonical scorecard\"","\"\\n| Factor | Score | Confidence / evidence | Reason | Contrary fact |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| GOV | 4 | Medium;E11,E12,E13,E15 | Three years of Section185 director-loan contraventions, repaid by FY23; later wording reports compliance but FY25 RPT loan direction is contradictory. Historical and current reliability issues require a hold, not an unproven diversion allegation. | Director loans were repaid by FY23 and AS15 was restated; lower remuneration and later compliance wording mitigate the historical findings. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Medium;E07,E08,E19,E23 | Qualified consumables and repeat orders, but no verified durable price premium, margin persistence, market share or switching-cost economics. | Approvals and repeat consumable demand plausibly create customer friction; no quantified premium or exclusivity was found. |\\n| REV | NS | Low;E02,E07,E09,E10 | Nominal audited growth is calculable but latest volume/price/mix bridge and commodity-adjusted economic growth cannot be established. | FY26 nominal revenue grew14.04%, but value added was nearly flat and current unit/price decomposition is missing. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Medium;E07,E10,E19 | Recurring consumables across fabrication/repair sectors, offset industrial cyclicality, top-ten concentration and export collapse. | Consumables recur across uses, but export contraction and concentrated customers can impair recurrence. |\\n| CAP | NS | Low;E08,E24,E25 | Broad incremental-return proxy deteriorates, but provision classification, doubtful AR, asset maturity and harmonized IC are material unresolved denominators; do not force a point score. | The Covid-base five-year proxy looks strong and CWIP may mature; neither resolves current asset/provision classifications or negative three-year incremental proxy. |\\n| CASH | 4 | Medium;E02,E04,E05,E14 | Audited ratio-of-sums plus persistent working-capital demands and recent negative FCF; FY26 provision-normalized conversion is worse, not a capex excuse for CFO. | Five-year reported CFO/PAT is0.77, and FY23 cash generation was strong; recent FCF and liability-supported FY26CFO are contrary evidence. |\\n| BAL | 6 | Medium;E05,E06,E14,E16,E20,E21 | Audited net cash and equity-funded expansion, subject to stale entry cash, claim settlement and intraperiod shortfall risks. | Low audited debt is real, but stale cash, opening liability settlements and uncertain facility availability limit its protection. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Medium;E08,E10,E11,E19 | Capacity/solar investment and lower remuneration are positives, but export contraction and lower incremental returns; utilization and commissioning outcomes unverified. | Expansion, solar and lower remuneration are positive execution evidence; measured utilization/payback and export continuity do not yet confirm it. |\\n| EARN | NS | Low;E02,E03,E04,E15 | Other income, allowance inconsistency, legacy restatement and unusual provisions prevent reliable organic normalized earnings-growth score. | Reported PAT rose in FY26; that does not resolve doubtful allowance or establish normalized recurring earnings growth. |\\n| R | NS | Low;E17,E20,E21,E22 | Conditional modeled returns cannot cure financial-reliability and entry-cash gaps. | Some bull paths survive without finance and reach break-even, but current evidence holds and hurdle tests prevent an investable point score. |\\n| D | 3 | Medium;E03,E04,E16,E18,E20 | Limited cash buffer under collection/capex stress, dilution and concentrated exit liquidity; not a price-derived favorable downside claim. | March net cash and repeat demand provide protection; they do not prevent collection/capex timing stress or SME exit constraints. |\\n| P | NS | Low;E17,E20,E22,E23 | No reliable conservative intrinsic-value margin of safety while G2 remains on hold. | Trailing18.18xPAT can look moderate; reliable normalized capital/cash and intrinsic-value support remain unavailable. |\\n| L | NS | Low;E18 | 21 missing security rows are not verified zero-volume days; no contemporaneous depth/circuit restriction evidence, and whole-lot participation fails observed-only diagnostics. | Observed trading and a19200-share latest session show some activity; missing sessions, lot size and absent live depth prevent executable liquidity scoring. |\\n\\n| Score | Raw/final | Coverage | Exact bound | Display bound |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | NS | 76.00% | [Decimal('3.54'), Decimal('5.70')] | ['3.5', '5.7'] |\\n| GQS | NS | 30.00% | [Decimal('2.10'), Decimal('8.40')] | ['2.1', '8.4'] |\\n| IAS | NS | 20.00% | [Decimal('1.40'), Decimal('8.60')] | ['1.4', '8.6'] |\\n\\nIndividual contributions below use the same canonical factors; NS is not a zero contribution. Evidence IDs resolve in evidence_ledger.json to public primary sources and precise locators.\\n\\n| Aggregate | Factor | Weight | Score | Weight × score |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| BQS | GOV | 0.20 | 4 | 0.8000 |\\n| BQS | MOAT | 0.20 | 4 | 0.8000 |\\n| BQS | REV | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | DUR | 0.10 | 5 | 0.5000 |\\n| BQS | CAP | 0.12 | NS | NS |\\n| BQS | CASH | 0.10 | 4 | 0.4000 |\\n| BQS | BAL | 0.08 | 6 | 0.4800 |\\n| BQS | EXEC | 0.08 | 4 | 0.3200 |\\n| GQS | REV | 0.40 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | DUR | 0.20 | 5 | 1.0000 |\\n| GQS | EARN | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | CAP | 0.15 | NS | NS |\\n| GQS | CASH | 0.10 | 4 | 0.4000 |\\n| IAS | Q | 0.25 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | R | 0.35 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | D | 0.20 | 3 | 0.6000 |\\n| IAS | P | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n| IAS | L | 0.10 | NS | NS |\\n\\nThe 13 canonical factors are reused, not independently rescaled for different aggregate scores. BQS weights are 20/20/12/10/12/10/8/8; GQS 40/20/15/15/10; IAS 25/35/20/10/10. Each sums to 100%. IAS Q is the **unrounded final BQS**, which is NS here; it is not replaced with the midpoint of the BQS range. For any incomplete score, bounds are known weighted contribution plus missing weight ×1 and ×10. Bounds are uncertainty ranges, not forecasts or substitute scores.\\n\\nNo numerical override is applied: GOV is 4 rather than ≤3, G1 does not fail, G3 is Conditional, and CASH=4 does not invoke the persistent-poor-cash ≤3 cap. G2 and G4 nevertheless make IAS NS and prohibit favorable investment classification. Display rounding is half-up to one decimal; underlying arithmetic and any future classification use exact, unrounded values. The bound is not a confidence interval. Factor sensitivity: MOAT4→3/5 shifts the BQS bound by−/+0.20 to3.34–5.50/3.74–5.90; BAL6→5/7 shifts it by−/+0.08 to3.46–5.62/3.62–5.78. GQS is unchanged for these two factors. IAS would move by0.05/0.02 respectively only if Q were fully scoreable; here IAS remainsNS and the gates do not change. This tests judgment sensitivity without converting uncertainty into a neutral score.\\n\\nSubject confidence: identity and direct statement extraction High; historical comparable earnings Medium/Low after restatements and contradictory notes; organic growth Low; capital returns Low; cash/funding at entry Low; governance resolution Medium/Low; execution Low. Forecast uncertainty is separate from whether a document was accurately read.\\n\\n\"","\"Business model and economic drivers\"","\"\\nRoyal Arc manufactures coated welding electrodes and flux-cored wire at Zaroli, Umbergaon, Valsad, Gujarat. Manufacturing entails wire preparation/drawing, powder/formulation mixing, extrusion or forming, baking, testing and packing. Inputs include steel wire/strip, stainless and nickel materials and ferro-alloy powders. Buyers use consumables in fabrication, repair and maintenance of structures, boilers, pressure vessels, pipes and industrial equipment. Quality failures can be costly, making product qualification and reliable supply valuable. This establishes a plausible commercial mechanism, not a proven price premium. [S03 pp143–151]\\n\\nThe published product catalogue is broader than actual in-house output. The prospectus says TIG/MIG production had stopped after FY21 because trading was commercially preferable. FY22–H1 FY25 capacity tables show nil TIG/MIG production, although machines could be reassigned. It would be wrong to treat catalogue breadth as evidence that all listed product categories were manufactured at scale in FY26. The latest comparable product-volume disclosure remains missing. [S03 pp145,153]\\n\\nFY24 pre-adjustment product sales comprised about 50.73% flux-cored wire, 39.73% electrode categories and 9.54% traded products. H1 FY25 manufacturing was 87.22% and trading 12.78%; TIG/MIG trading was 10.50%. These are product-sales denominators, not automatically the audited revenue denominator. The product table reconciles via freight, rate differences and discounts, with minor residual differences to the financial revenue note. [S03 pp145–147]\\n\\nCash is not earned when an order is mentioned. Materials and staff precede finished goods, shipment creates receivables, and collections may lag. Customer advances are modest relative to receivables. FY26 trade receivables of ₹25.75 crore, inventory ₹13.1826  crore and supplier advances ₹4.6877 crore substantially exceed trade payables ₹1.5274  crore and customer advances ₹0.9257 crore. This is a working-capital-intensive business even with little reported bank debt. [S01 notes7,14–17]\\n\\nRaw materials consumed represented 76.90%, 66.72%, 63.97% and 79.14% of revenue in FY22, FY23, FY24 and H1 FY25 respectively. Changes in product mix, input costs and inventory therefore matter enormously. No universal contractual pass-through formula or fixed pass-through lag has been demonstrated. Higher metal prices can increase invoice revenue and funding needs without increasing economic value added. [S03 p144]\\n\\n\"","\"Competitive position and moat\"","\"\\n**Qualification mechanism:** approved grades and customer testing may reduce willingness to switch in critical applications. Evidence is management's ABS/IBR/BIS and quality-testing disclosures; durability depends on consistent quality and repeat approvals. Failure mode: an already qualified competitor offers equivalent performance with better service/credit. Certification is not evidence of exclusivity or market leadership. [S03 p143]\\n\\n**Formulation and service mechanism:** grade selection, customized formulations and application support may improve retention. Evidence is product breadth and in-house testing, but customer-level retention, price realization and failure-rate data are ND. Failure mode: commoditized grades, price competition and product substitution. No unsupported “top three” market-share claim is used.\\n\\n**Scale/distribution mechanism:** repeat dealer and industrial relationships can reduce customer acquisition effort, but Royal Arc lacks the verified scale and technology economics of a much larger peer such as ESAB. The latest concentration table has top-ten customers at 44.19% in FY24 and 47.84% in H1 FY25; top customer 8.75% and 10.65%. Diversified end-use descriptions do not eliminate revenue concentration. [S03 customer table pp157–159]\\n\\nA capable entrant needs formulation skills, quality systems, commercial relationships and working capital, not merely a basic production line. Yet there is no quantified entrant payback, switching-cost premium or durable excess-margin series. Hence MOAT=4. The thesis should be tested with repeat-customer volumes, customer churn, realized prices relative to input costs, rejection rates and contribution per tonne, not a list of certifications.\\n\\n\"","\"Revenue growth and earnings quality\"","\"\\nAll figures below are ₹crore. FY19–FY21 are original audited AS reporting; FY22–24 use the prospectus restatement; FY25–26 use the latest audited statements. FY22–26 capex marked * is the published net cash purchase/sale line, not a fabricated gross purchase number; FY19–21 show gross purchases, with FY19/FY20 disposals separately retained. Operating EBIT excludes other income and adds finance costs; FY25's ₹0.4976 crore exceptional expense remains in the conservative reported operating bridge.\\n\\n| FY | Revenue | PAT | CFO | Cash capex* | FCF | Operating EBIT |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY19 | 75.79 | 1.26 | 5.22 | 0.29 | 4.93 | 3.35 |\\n| FY20 | 64.56 | 1.19 | 6.03 | 0.21 | 5.82 | 1.79 |\\n| FY21 | 43.32 | 1.57 | 7.32 | 2.37 | 4.94 | 1.06 |\\n| FY22 | 62.91 | 2.12 | 2.40 | 1.14 | 1.27 | 1.87 |\\n| FY23 | 96.94 | 9.57 | 12.59 | 1.17 | 11.41 | 12.57 |\\n| FY24 | 99.79 | 11.93 | 5.63 | 5.08 | 0.54 | 15.15 |\\n| FY25 | 101.77 | 9.01 | 4.83 | 7.76 | -2.93 | 11.68 |\\n| FY26 | 116.06 | 10.05 | 7.53 | 11.42 | -3.89 | 11.91 |\\n\\n| FY | Equity | Debt incl.current | Total cash | Fixed incl.CWIP | AR | Inventory | IC proxy | EPS end-share adjusted |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| FY19 | 16.10 | 20.37 | 2.45 | 16.35 | 14.09 | 14.88 | 34.00 | 1.35 |\\n| FY20 | 17.29 | 12.20 | 0.73 | 14.82 | 10.81 | 10.90 | 28.75 | 1.28 |\\n| FY21 | 18.87 | 8.23 | 0.30 | 15.51 | 10.65 | 8.74 | 26.77 | 1.69 |\\n| FY22 | 20.78 | 7.81 | 0.05 | 14.88 | 13.09 | 10.84 | 28.52 | 2.28 |\\n| FY23 | 30.35 | 1.42 | 3.02 | 13.96 | 16.44 | 6.07 | 27.39 | 10.29 |\\n| FY24 | 42.27 | 0.20 | 0.96 | 17.24 | 20.68 | 9.04 | 39.29 | 12.82 |\\n| FY25 | 71.78 | 0.81 | 19.30 | 22.05 | 20.93 | 14.28 | 51.81 | 8.11 |\\n| FY26 | 81.27 | 0.20 | 14.49 | 29.94 | 25.75 | 13.18 | 65.10 | 9.05 |\\n\\nCapital and per-share inputs are shown above; fully diluted issued shares equal the basic issued count at the dated no-convertible disclosure. End-share EPS is a comparable denominator diagnostic, not reported weighted-average EPS. The February 2024 bonus was 411 for every 100 shares, factor 5.11, not a cash-funded dilution; the February 2025 IPO fresh shares were dilution. Exact EBITDA, reported/normalized PAT, net debt, ROE, financing-capital ROIC, receivable/inventory/payable days and cash-cycle proxies are in financial_metrics.json. Inventory/payable days use material-plus-inventory-change cost as a disclosed proxy, not exact credit purchases or fully allocated manufacturing COGS. Original-period leases and contract assets were not separately disclosed; no material hidden lease is assumed away. No acquisition was identified in the reviewed standalone history. [S03 capital history; S01 financial notes]\\n\\nRevenue grew from ₹43.3228 crore in pandemic-affected FY21 to ₹116.06 crore in FY26. The five-year CAGR is mechanically high and base-distorted; it is not the preferred organic-growth anchor. The FY23–26 three-year revenue CAGR is much lower, about 6.2%. FY19/FY20 original revenue was ₹75.7875/₹64.5624 crore, reinforcing the FY21 base distortion and showing earlier contraction rather than a straight growth line. FY19/FY20 cash capex is gross and disposals are separately retained; original total debt includes current maturities in other current liabilities, not just borrowing headings. [S13–S14] The complete exact CAGR calculations and annual absolute additions are in the calculation artifact. A ten-year CAGR is unavailable because no verified eleven-observation comparable annual series was assembled; no nine- or eight-year series is relabelled ten years. A four-year restated series is not misrepresented as a five-year CAGR. [S01–S07]\\n\\nThe original FY22/FY23/FY24 revenue figures were ₹63.2553/₹97.3190/₹100.1073 crore versus restated ₹62.9097/₹96.9402/₹99.7875 crore. Original PAT was ₹2.1212/₹9.6955/₹12.1282 crore versus restated ₹2.1213/₹9.5682/₹11.9263 crore. Reclassifications, gratuity and other adjustments must remain visible. The prospectus restatement bridge contains its own confusing total; use the financial statements and retain the bridge, not a favorable unexplained splice. [S03 pp201–239; S04–S06]\\n\\n**Economic cross-check:** revenue less materials consumed and finished-goods/WIP inventory change, a value-added proxy rather than audited gross profit, was approximately ₹30.04 crore in FY23, ₹33.86 crore in FY24, ₹32.31 crore in FY25 and ₹32.34 crore in FY26. It excludes staff and other factory costs, so it must not be labelled true gross profit. FY26 nominal sales rose sharply without a corresponding value-added increase. No FY25/FY26 unit/price/mix bridge is disclosed in the sources reviewed; REV remains NS rather than applying the revenue-growth scoring ladder to nominal commodity invoices. [S01, S03]\\n\\nExports fell from ₹17.0083 crore in FY25 to ₹3.8904 crore in FY26, about 77.1%. Current broad claims about export opportunity and countries served must not override this realized contraction. Domestic growth may offset exports in the total, but profitability and customer quality of that replacement mix are unknown. [S01 note32]\\n\\nFY26 other income was ₹1.9435 crore, including ₹0.8735 crore interest and ₹0.9001 crore FX gains. Operating EBIT is ₹11.9127 crore versus reported PBT ₹13.618 crore, and normalized-tax operating NOPAT is ₹8.9345 crore before financing. Other income is not automatically recurring operating growth; FX treatment is disclosed rather than blindly called extraordinary. FY25 PAT of ₹9.0056 crore also bears the exceptional expense and a shorter weighted-average share denominator. FY26 reported EPS ₹9.05 versus FY25 ₹9.49 is not evidence of declining total PAT: IPO share timing changed weighted average shares. At current issued shares, FY25 PAT implies about ₹8.11/share. [S01 statements and notes19,29]\\n\\nH1 FY26 revenue ₹52.8390  crore and PAT ₹4.3146 crore compare with H1 FY25 restated ₹45.6047  crore and ₹3.1750 crore. They show improvement but are already included in FY26. There is no claim that these data constitute current FY27 momentum. [S08, S03]\\n\\n\"","\"Growth runway and reinvestment economics\"","\"\\nThe prospectus's installed annual electrode capacity was 10,800 tonnes; FY24 production 3,459 tonnes, or 32.03%. Flux-cored-wire capacity increased to 7,200 tonnes, FY24 production 2,879 tonnes, or 39.99%. H1 FY25 production was 1,951 and 1,500 tonnes respectively; annualized utilization 36.13% and 41.67%. FY22/FY23 electrode production was 3,522/2,944 tonnes and flux-cored wire 1,477/2,624 tonnes. This is uneven volume history, not automatic uninterrupted compounding. [S03 p153]\\n\\nCrucially, the denominator assumed 24-hour production while actual operations generally used an eight-hour shift plus overtime, with quality/process constraints described by management. The apparent two-thirds spare capacity is therefore not entirely free capacity available at unchanged cost. Extra shifts need labor, process control, working capital and orders. Product-interchangeable lines cannot each be counted at full simultaneous capacity. FY26 commissioning, line-level uptime, output and saleable tonnage remain ND. [S03 pp152–154]\\n\\nFY26 PPE/intangibles/CWIP reached ₹29.9424 crore, including ₹5.4314 crore CWIP, from ₹22.0496 crore. Cash capex was ₹11.416 crore. The difference between cash capex less depreciation and the fixed-asset change requires the disclosed asset movement/disposal/noncash bridge; the model exposes the residual instead of pretending cash capex equals net asset additions. The plant/solar investment may improve future output and energy cost, but an installation announcement is not a measured payback. FY26 chairman wording refers to 950 “KWh” solar versus prior kWp references; no precise energy-saving return is inferred from inconsistent units. [S01 fixed-asset schedule and chairman statement]\\n\\nThe accounting financing-capital proxy is equity plus interest-bearing debt less total cash and investments. It rose from ₹27.3892 crore in FY23 to ₹65.0967 crore in FY26, while normalized-tax operating NOPAT fell from ₹9.4254 crore to ₹8.9345 crore. The observed three-year incremental proxy is approximately **−1.30%**. This is not a project IRR and not proof every recent machine destroys value. It includes CWIP and is affected by tax/provision financing, doubtful assets, restatements and timing. A clean organic committed-capital denominator is not currently established, so CAP remains NS. [S01, S03; calculation bridge]\\n\\nThe model retains two-, three- and four-year changes, finance-side reconciliation, cumulative capex less D&A, fixed-capital change and residual net operating-capital change. The five-year original-basis numerical proxy is also provided, using ₹26.7744 crore FY21 financing capital after adding current debt maturities. It is strongly positive because it begins with depressed Covid-era NOPAT, unlike the negative three-year proxy. It is **not a harmonized five-year organic project return**: the gratuity/restatement and operating-liability basis differ. CAP remains NS; the contrast is a base/timing warning, not a reason to choose the favorable window. A commissioned-only return would be falsely flattering if CWIP were simply removed without matching the associated earnings. Required evidence is commissioning dates, actual utilization, incremental gross contribution, maintenance capex and a reconciled operating balance sheet. Compare prospective post-tax returns with a reasoned 14–18% nominal INR small-company capital-cost range, not a single unexamined universal WACC.\\n\\n\"","\"Governance, ownership and execution\"","\"\\nControl is concentrated in the Sanghvi promoter family: 8,099,178 shares or 72.96% at 31 March 2026, with 27.04% public. The same filing reports no promoter pledge/NDU/other encumbrance and no outstanding warrants, convertibles or ESOPs; 2,258,900 shares (20.35% of total) remain locked in. These are dated declarations, not current October assertions. The latest half-year ownership update was not located by the cutoff. [S12] The IPO had 18 lakh new shares and 12 lakh OFS shares at ₹120: company gross fresh cash ₹21.6 crore, promoter/OFS proceeds ₹14.4 crore. The ₹36 crore offer headline is not company funding. Net fresh cash was approximately ₹20.4611 crore after issue costs. Pre-IPO 9,300,200 shares plus 1,800,000 new shares reconcile to 11,100,200. No bonus share is treated as economic dilution. [S03; S01 share capital; official security master]\\n\\nFY26 RPT director/family remuneration plus rent was approximately ₹1.9359 crore, about 19.3% of PAT. Remuneration decreased from the prior year and a director salary reduction was disclosed, which is a favorable development, but related rents and appointments still warrant minority scrutiny. Reappointments approved during 2026 must not be mistaken for evidence that future operating targets will be met. [S01 RPT/remuneration; June board and AGM notices]\\n\\n**Original audit chronology must survive the IPO restatement.** FY21's emphasis wording explicitly says the gratuity liability was not ascertained/provided and the opinion was modified in that respect. The original FY19 and FY20 audits contain the same wording. FY20 reports Section185/186 compliance and no director loans; the repeated loan contravention is documented in FY21–23, not retroactively attributed to FY20. [S13 p7; S14 pp4,7] FY22/FY23 original records show further actuarial-policy versus actual-provision inconsistency; FY23 note37 says no present/future gratuity liability was ascertained or provided despite the accounting policy saying actuarial provision/funding. The prospectus explicitly retrospectively adjusts AS15 liabilities/expenses in FY22–24, including equity adjustments of ₹0.4063 crore in FY22 and ₹0.5858 crore in FY23. This is positive remediation. Its examiner relies on earlier audits and does not reissue them. Later restatement and booked gratuity do not make the original reports clean. The restatement bridge also has conflicting signs/totals, so the controlling restated statements are retained rather than inventing corrections. [S05–S07; original FY19 audit p7; S03 restatement]\\n\\nFY21 CARO reports director loans contrary to Section185 with maximum outstanding ₹3.2691624 crore. FY22 repeats the finding at ₹4.457956 crore. FY23 repeats it at ₹1.7163102  crore and explicitly says the loans were repaid by year-end. Repayment resolves that balance, not the three-year control history. FY24 reports Sections185/186 compliance. FY25 says no loans/investments in CARO, but the RPT note labels ₹4 lakh “Loan given during the year” to a director; its direction needs reconciliation with director borrowing movements. It is therefore too strong to declare there were definitely no later loans. [S07 p7; S06 p8; S05 p8; S04 p43; S02 pp70,97]\\n\\nFY22/FY23 bank quarterly AR/AP returns differed from books, with no quarterly stock valuation system. FY24 still shows differences and the same stock issue. FY25 CARO does **not** certify reconciliation: it says no sanctioned working-capital limits above ₹5 crore, while security/personal-guarantee notes reference larger amounts. FY26 CARO says facilities above ₹5  crore and returns agree. The changes may reflect different limits or reporting, but no full historical reconciliation is supplied. [S04 p42; S02 p69; S01 CARO]\\n\\nThe prior statutory auditor resigned in October 2024; successor CARO describes appointment and no outgoing concerns. This is not automatically misconduct, nor should the absence of concerns erase prior adverse findings. FY26 unmodified opinion and effective-IFC conclusion are positive but do not override contradictions in notes. FY21/FY22 IFC reporting exemption must not be rewritten as an affirmative clean IFC audit. [S02 p72; S06–S07; S01]\\n\\nDated delivery record, separating spending from economic delivery:\\n\\n| Dated statement / observable milestone | Expected evidence | Observed delivery by cutoff | Assessment |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n|18Feb2025 IPO objects: fund expansion and working capital [S03]|Use-of-proceeds and commissioning, then output/collection evidence|FY26 utilization reports₹4.8866cr expansion and₹14cr working capital; CWIP₹5.4314cr at31Mar2026 [S01]|Funding deployed, not demonstrated project payback; line-level commissioning/utilization ND.|\\n|FY25/FY26 solar investment commentary [S02/S01]|Installed nameplate units, generation, energy-cost saving and capex return|Earlier450kWp versus later950 “KWh” wording|Installation claimed; units and realized savings not reconciled. No quantified payback target verified.|\\n|FY26 export growth/market opportunity narrative [S01]|Exports, contribution margin and collections|FY26 exports₹3.8904cr versusFY25₹17.0083cr|Realized contraction contradicts a simple expansion interpretation; no numerical future export promise verified.|\\n|18Feb2025 restatement AS15 adjustment [S03]|Actuarial liabilities and expense recognized consistently|Retrospective adjustment plus later gratuity provisions|Positive accounting remediation, with reconciliation glitches and original modified opinions retained.|\\n|1Oct2025 remuneration reduction [S01]|Lower actual related director remuneration|FY26 director/family salaries declined; rent remained related|Favorable delivery but not proof of improved operating capital returns.|\\n\\nExecution assessment is mixed: expansion and solar investment, reduced remuneration and listed-company disclosure are positives; realized export contraction, lower incremental productivity and contradictory disclosure quality prevent a high EXEC score. Management statements about recurring customers or new opportunities remain M evidence until actual volume, price and cash outcomes verify them.\\n\\n\"","\"Financial forensics, cash and funding\"","\"\\n**Receivables:** FY26 note15 lists ₹2.9709 crore as undisputed but considered doubtful, versus ₹1.3266 crore prior year, while the allowance is nil versus ₹0.0581 crore. The doubtful balance equals about 29.6% of FY26 PAT. It could reflect a labelling or accounting error, but neither possibility permits treating it as unquestionably good. Obtain age-wise customer ledgers, subsequent bank collections, credit notes and the auditor's signed reconciliation. A modest one-line ageing caveat is inadequate. [S01 p92]\\n\\n**Provisions:** other short provisions rose from ₹0.2303 crore to ₹4.2034 crore. Total short-provision growth contributed about ₹4.1466 crore to CFO. Reported CFO was ₹7.528 crore; removing only the increment in other provisions gives ₹3.5549 crore, about 35.4% of PAT. This is an analytical sensitivity, not a replacement audited CFO or proof the liability will all settle immediately. Nature, reversals, expense counterpart and settlement dates are ND. The cash model separately pays this opening liability, rather than including it in working capital and then paying it twice. [S01 notes9 and cash flow]\\n\\n**Cash conversion:** the five-year FY22–26 ratio of summed CFO to summed PAT is approximately 0.77, and the three-year ratio is approximately 0.58. Exact sums, rather than averages of annual ratios, are retained. Inventory release helped FY23 CFO; increasing receivables, advances and falling payables hurt later years. FY25/FY26 reported FCF was −₹2.9297/−₹3.8880 crore. Capex explains the gap from CFO to FCF, not weak CFO itself. Interest is financing-classified in the published cash flow; analytical FCFF is separately constructed. [S01–S03]\\n\\n**Cash classification:** FY26 ₹14.4901 crore total cash/bank includes ₹0.0465 crore margin money under lien, with the published cash-equivalent subtotal(A) ₹14.4435 crore used as potentially accessible at 31 March. Rounded line items and totals differ by₹0.01 lakh; the model uses that published subtotal rather than inventing precision from subtracting rounded totals. The ₹2.447 crore favorable debit cash-credit balance is cash, not borrowing. Gross debt is ₹0.2038 crore. A cash-credit label must not cause cash and debt to be counted simultaneously. Noncurrent deposits and non-operating investments are not silently made spendable cash. [S01 note16]\\n\\n**Investments:** note12 includes ₹1.4566 crore investment property, cooperative shares and ₹0.4061 crore newly acquired quoted Cochin/L&T shares, yet CARO says no investments were made. The property is non-operating; it is excluded from base EV cash deduction and has no forecast disposal proceeds. An alternative book-value deduction is disclosed only as a valuation sensitivity. The property must never be counted both in operating returns and again as excess cash. [S01 notes12 and CARO]\\n\\n**Claims:** FY26's nil contingency table coexists with an ongoing FY19–20 GST show-cause notice of ₹0.3346 crore, described as unascertained. FY24 had VAT ₹1.3025 crore, GST notices, unquantified income/local-body tax issues and a June2024 TDS amount. Later generic no-dispute/no-litigation paragraphs do not establish each claim's settlement. The base model includes only disclosed booked claims and separately tests cash stresses; unquantified legal exposure remains ND, not zero. [S04 note30; S01 note30]\\n\\n**IPO and asset funding:** FY25 financing cash flow included fresh equity, not organically generated cash. FY26 cash decreased ₹4.8131 crore, from ₹19.3032 crore to ₹14.4901 crore: CFO ₹7.528 crore, investing outflow ₹10.9249  crore and financing outflow ₹1.4162 crore reconcile. Investing includes capex, investments and interest; financing includes debt repayment, interest and ₹0.555 crore interim dividend. The FY26 final ₹0.50/share dividend has a record date before our entry and is separately modelled in the March-to-entry bridge. The interim already reflected in audited cash is not deducted again. [S01 statements; AGM]\\n\\nIPO utilization reported expansion ₹4.8866 crore, working capital ₹14  crore and GCP approximately ₹2.5852 crore, leaving ₹0.1282 crore against gross fresh proceeds. Gross-versus-net issue costs must reconcile before treating that residual as incremental free cash. The model starts from audited bank cash, not audited cash plus IPO proceeds again. Guarantees or historic sanction amounts do not establish available October bank headroom. A current issuer-specific rating rationale was not located in issuer/disclosed-source and public rating searches; no inferred external credit rating is assigned. [S01 IPO utilization]\\n\\n\"","\"Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\"","\"\\nAt the reference price, equity value is ₹182.59829 crore. Headline trailing P/E is about 18.18×; price/book about 2.25×. Using March accessible cash and debt, EV before any property/investment monetization is ₹168.35859 crore, about 14.13× operating EBIT and 10.91× operating EBITDA. These are cross-date diagnostics, not current executable fair value. Replacing operating earnings with total income or netting pledged cash would make them misleadingly attractive. [S01 and official market extracts]\\n\\n**SME market-maker overlay:** The prospectus names Shreni Shares Limited under the5December2024 market-making agreement, with153,600 offer shares reserved and a stated three-year commitment from listing. The March2026 shareholding filing shows Shreni holding630,000 shares,5.68% of total. This concentration and contractual role do not prove current two-way quote depth or guarantee an exit; current market-maker inventory and actual firm bid/ask quotes are ND. [S03 market-making section; S12]\\n\\n**Execution diagnostic:** 60 scheduled sessions from 24 July through 19 October contain 39 Royal rows and 21 absent security rows; 14 of 39 observed sessions have a single traded price. Observed-only median turnover is ₹5.46 lakh and mean about ₹13.06 lakh. At 2%/5%/10% of the lower observed-only statistic, the rounded-up position needs 54.23/21.69/10.85 fractional-session equivalents, but each participation allowance buys less than one 1,200-share lot. Thus a strict daily whole-lot implementation has zero permitted lots at all three rates. Missing rows are not proven zero-volume sessions, and one-price sessions alone do not prove circuit-locks. The master price band/status fields are retained, not decoded into an unsupported current restriction assurance. The genuine 60-session exit estimate and L remain NS. Recent20-session evidence is similarly thin:12 observed and8 absent rows, observed mean₹974,220, median₹450,930 and4 one-price sessions. The recent observed-only median is lower, not an improving-depth signal.\\n\\n**Model specification:** seven unequal April–19 October pre-entry periods are cash-linked to the audited March balance sheet, then sixty monthly ownership periods. No current cash balance is asserted. Each month has capex, opening claim settlements, finance costs, four supplier-before-customer collection cycles and tax. Supplier prepayment is 70% of modeled operating cash cost per cycle; 1/2/4/8-cycle sensitivities show the dependence on collection timing. A ₹1 crore minimum cash buffer is required at every event. Negative receipts due to collection/asset loss assumptions are not artificially floored away.\\n\\nYear-one sales use FY26 revenue ×(1+growth), not a fabricated interim report. Bear/base/bull sales growth is −5%/8%/15%; EBITDA margins 10%/13.30%/16%; DSO 120/90/75; maintenance capex 3.5% and D&A 3% of sales; fixed-capital target 30% of revenue. Working-capital targets explicitly include receivables, inventory, supplier advances, other assets, trade payables, customer advances and other operating liabilities. Growth capex is the extra capital needed after maintenance less D&A, so an extra maintenance amount is not counted again as growth capacity.\\n\\nPre-entry annual sales paces are ₹105/122/130  crore and capex ₹8/6/5 crore. These are analyst stress assumptions, not reported FY27 guidance. Doubtful-receivable losses are full/half/nil. Opening other provisions are settled over four months in the principal cases; settlement of zero/half/all is separately tested. Loss reduces collections consistently with the receivable bridge; it is not both an extra cash payment and a collection deduction. No tax benefit on the doubtful loss is assumed.\\n\\nExisting party debt is paid in the first pre-entry month, a stated conservative timing assumption. Opening long-term employee provisions ₹0.6940 crore and long-term deposits ₹0.2371 crore are conservatively settled in the first ownership month; they are outside ONWC and are not allowed to vanish at terminal. No cash timing for deferred tax is fabricated beyond normalized ongoing cash taxation. The optional financing case allows at most ₹5 crore uncommitted bank borrowing at 13%, then post-entry equity at ₹100/share (₹60 in the bear) with 5% fees, maximum ₹10 crore gross and the 18,000,000 authorized-share ceiling. Shares are whole numbers rounded upward to cover net proceeds. No pre-entry issue is permitted because the observed entry share count is fixed. Optional financing is sought **before** a payment would breach cash; if a shortfall still occurs, failure is irreversible. Subsequent rows continue only to expose the complete diagnostic economic path, never to restore an ordinary return.\\n\\n| Case / funding | Entry cash | Cash survives | First breach period | Optional bank | New shares | Exit price | Net CAGR |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| bear / no_new_finance | -10.63 | False | 3 | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS% |\\n| bear / bounded_optional | -5.79 | False | 5 | 5.00 | 0 | NS | NS% |\\n| base / no_new_finance | 2.60 | False | 8 | 0.00 | 0 | NS | NS% |\\n| base / bounded_optional | 2.60 | True | None | 1.56 | 0 | 82.50 | -13.59% |\\n| bull / no_new_finance | 13.16 | True | None | 0.00 | 0 | 187.30 | 1.81% |\\n| bull / bounded_optional | 13.16 | True | None | 0.00 | 0 | 187.30 | 1.81% |\\n\\nThe no-new-finance base can have positive year-end cash yet fail in the first ownership month's supplier-before-collection cycle. This is exactly why annual-only financing checks are insufficient. Optional base liquidity is conditional on a bank advance, not an established available facility. The bear fails even with the bounded bank case before entry; no later paper equity value is presented as a realizable return.\\n\\n**Earned terminal economics:** terminal value is not a chosen promotional P/E. Next-year NOPAT plus D&A, less maintenance capex, capacity growth, working-capital growth and required-buffer growth produces terminal FCFF. Discount rates are 18%/16%/14%, long-run growth 2%/4%/5% in bear/base/bull. The code rejects discount≤growth. A twelve-month terminal cash schedule settles all debt once, tests supplier/collection timing with no new finance and solves the retained operating reserve including its own growth. Equity value is EV plus cash less debt less that reserve. This is one debt claim, not duplicate terminal repayments; restricted cash and property are not added again. Ordinary return is NS if pre-entry, intraperiod or terminal survival fails.\\n\\nThe model reports terminal implied EV/EBIT and ROIC. Separate 8/10/12× EBIT cross-checks are diagnostics, not permission to substitute an unearned higher terminal multiple. The terminal asset target accounts for maintenance less D&A before requiring incremental fixed capital, consistent with explicit-period reinvestment. A high growth assumption that requires unfinanceable capital is rejected rather than rewarded with a large exit value.\\n\\nAll returns use diluted terminal shares, 2% entry costs and 2% exit costs, and no invented post-entry dividend distribution. Cash retained belongs to terminal shareholders; actual pre-entry dividends do not accrue to a new buyer. Transaction-cost assumptions do not solve whole-lot/depth constraints. Explicit zero/2%/5% friction sensitivities are stored for every surviving path; they are not assumed broker fee quotations.\\n\\nEach path also stores required gross exit price and terminal EV/EBIT to reach 12%/15%/18% after costs. These hurdle arithmetic checks remain labelled unattainable where the cash path fails, rather than implying that a richer multiple funds an earlier cash shortfall.\\n\\n**Reverse expectations:** growth is scanned from 0% through 40% in 1 percentage-point steps for base/bull economics and both financing modes, including the literal 0% price-implied break-even return and 12%/15%/18% hurdles, then feasible sign-changing hurdle brackets are bisected 48 times. A bracket cannot jump across a failed path. Every evaluated path, including failures and root intermediates, is retained and losslessly deduplicated by full input specification. “No root in this domain” does not prove no mathematical root exists elsewhere.\\n\\n| Case | Funding | Hurdle | Result |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 0% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | no_new_finance | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 0% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| base | bounded_optional | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 0% | 10.2798% to 10.2798% |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | no_new_finance | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 0% | 10.2798% to 10.2798% |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 12.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 15.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n| bull | bounded_optional | 18.00% | No feasible sign-changing bracket in stated domain; not proof none outside domain |\\n\\nThere are 366 unique full cash paths in the canonical archive. The two-way growth/margin grid, discount/terminal-growth grid, DSO, opening capex, liability settlement, borrowing cap, issue-price, maintenance and collection-cycle sensitivities are recoverable from the compact inputs and code. Financial reliability, rather than a chosen root, remains the binding gate.\\n\\n**Primary economic peers:** these are economic comparisons, not same-date price-multiple anchors.\\n\\n| Company | FY26 revenue | Normalized EBIT margin | CFO/PAT | FCF |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| ESAB India | 1508.15 | 16.76% | 0.87 | 149.14 |\\n| GEE Limited | 369.1388 | 8.01% | -0.60 | -20.62 |\\n| Ador Welding | 1135.45 | 9.00% | 1.41 | 94.57 |\\n\\nESAB is much larger, with equipment, technology, group relationships and distribution advantages. Source-vintage limitation: the3July original was reconciled to the7July primary corrigendum/revised report, whose cover says no financial-statement impact and whose P&L/cash-flow numeric tokens match. A later29July revision was identified, but its primary attachment returned403; its full changes were not independently inspected. No claim of latest-version peer-audit review is made. [S11/S16] Its FY26 exceptional net gain ₹17.26 crore includes land gain ₹30.91 crore less labour-code expense ₹13.65 crore; the EBIT comparison removes the net exceptional gain and other income. GEE's rebound follows a FY25 loss, with negative FY26 CFO, asset-sale proceeds ₹30.442  crore and warrant cash ₹10.20 crore. It illustrates capital/cash risk, not a clean superior quality benchmark. Royal's EBIT margin lies between these businesses, but its receivable/provision reliability is unresolved. No peer's multiple is borrowed as a Royal target without normalized mix, cash and same-date primary price evidence. [S10 pp76–80; S11 pp106–108,145]\\n\\nAdor is the third verified economic peer, using its official revised audited FY26 results rather than the annual PDF whose retrieval timed out. Its mechanical pre-exception operating EBIT excludes other income but retains ₹24.82 crore onerous/LD costs and ₹14.12 crore historic Kuwait receivable recovery. Removing both would produce a different hypothetical margin; neither is silently normalized away. Welding segment revenue/result were ₹1,078.88/₹164.30crore, while flares/process were ₹56.71/−₹23.06crore. CFO benefited from ₹84.49 crore payables growth and the recovery. Prior merger and current subsidiary impairment also complicate trend comparisons. [S15 pp5–10]\\n\\n\"","\"Strongest bear case and failure path\"","\"\\nThe strongest bear case is not merely a lower market multiple. It is that FY26 growth represents lower-value-added domestic invoices replacing exports, while cash realization is flattered by liability creation and doubtful receivables. Capital is already committed to fixed assets, inventory and supplier advances. A large customer pays late, provisions settle and expansion consumes cash before collections arrive. The reported March net-cash label then disappears at an intraperiod trough; a new buyer cannot assume later annual profits will bridge it.\\n\\nConcentration matters: the latest disclosed top-ten share approaches half sales, while current customer-level profitability is ND. Input-price increases can require additional working capital before customer repricing; low-quality receivables can absorb PAT. A continued shortage can force expensive debt or equity below the purchase price. SME trading lots and one-price sessions can prevent an exit precisely when the thesis fails.\\n\\nThe thesis-break variable is **cash collectible from the disputed/doubtful receivable population and the settlement nature/timing of other provisions**, followed by incremental contribution per unit of capital. A reconciled auditor explanation could improve reliability; continued unexplained contradictions, repeat director advances contrary to law or refusal to quantify cash obligations would deepen the gate problem. No specific wrongdoing is assumed without evidence.\\n\\n\"","\"Multibagger and compounder test\"","\"\\nA doubling over five years requires about 14.87% annualized gross return, already close to the 15% hurdle before costs. Tripling requires about 24.57%. This cannot be justified by revenue CAGR alone. The model demands per-share value creation after reinvestment, operating reserves, debt and any new shares. Earnings funded by recurring equity issuance do not compound for the original shareholder at the company growth rate.\\n\\nThe favorable operating ingredients are repeat consumable demand, qualified products, a manufacturing base and potentially better utilization. The necessary evidence is missing: current real volumes/price realization, stable normalized margins, reliable collectible receivables, a reconciled provision book and attractive mature incremental returns. Apparent spare 24-hour capacity and a large addressable infrastructure market do not fill those gaps.\\n\\nThere is no compounder designation under v3.0. BQS/GQS are incomplete, governance/control history remains relevant, financial reliability is on hold and executable liquidity has not been established. The model may generate conditional high-growth paths, but those are boundary tests, not a probability-weighted multibagger claim.\\n\\n\"","\"Monitoring plan and explicit resolution tests\"","\"\\n1. Obtain the signed reconciliation of FY26 note15 doubtful classification and nil allowance, plus subsequent collections. Upgrade G2 only if the material balance and earnings effect reconcile, not because one total matches.\\n2. Identify each component of ₹4.2034 crore other provisions, expense counterpart, reversal/settlement, cash dates and related parties. Re-run entry cash and all intraperiod paths, not just terminal PAT.\\n3. Reconcile CARO no-investment wording with the securities purchases, FY25 RPT loan direction, and all open tax notices. Preserve prior audit findings rather than deleting them from the update history.\\n4. Obtain FY27 interim results and cash flow when publicly released; reconstruct March-to-entry cash using actual data and compare to scenario assumptions. This report does not claim an already scheduled monitoring service.\\n5. Track tonnes by product, manufacturing versus trading, realizations, material-cost lag, contribution per tonne, saleable capacity, shifts, utilization and commissioning dates. Only then consider REV/CAP/EARN point scores.\\n6. Verify committed available bank limits, covenants, security and debt schedules. Historic personal guarantees are not available cash.\\n7. Track customer concentration, receivable ageing, supplier advances, inventory days and payables. Three consecutive credible reporting periods with improved collections would be stronger than a one-off CFO spike.\\n8. Obtain all 60-session security-status/zero-trade evidence and live permitted-order depth before any execution conclusion. Respect actual lot size and market restrictions; do not assume fractional-lot liquidity.\\n9. Recompute valuation from a contemporaneous price and cash anchor. A lower price can improve IAS inputs only after gates permit scoring; it cannot make unreliable accounts reliable.\\n\\nMonitoring thresholds below are analyst decision rules, not company guidance, covenants or automatic factor caps. “Next filing” means the next publicly available statutory interim/annual record, not an assumed release date.\\n\\n| Item and dated baseline | Expected evidence / milestone | Warning threshold | Thesis-break / gate consequence |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n|31Mar2026 doubtfulAR₹2.9709cr, allowance nil [S01]|Signed auditor reconciliation and customer-wise subsequent collections at next filing|Material balance still unexplained or collection evidence absent|Proven large unprovided loss plus inability to fund obligations; escalateG2 and evaluateG3, never dismiss as rounding.|\\n|31Mar2026 other provisions₹4.2034cr [S01]|Nature, counterpart expense, related party and dated cash settlement schedule|Another material unexplained increase/reversal or missing schedule|Evidence that cash/earnings were materially misstated or obligations cannot be paid; retain/escalate reliability hold.|\\n|31Mar2026 AR₹25.75cr and supplier advances₹4.6877cr [S01]|H1FY27 cash conversion and bank collection bridge|DSO above90 days for two comparable reporting observations, or advances rise without deliveries|DSO approaches120 days with the model's unfunded cash trough; financing resilience thesis breaks unless committed cash is documented.|\\n|FY26 value added₹32.34cr, nearly flat [S01]|Product tonnes, realization, material lag and contribution per tonne|Sales grow but value added/collections again fail to grow|Sustained capital growth without corresponding operatingNOPAT/FCF invalidates the reinvestment thesis.|\\n|31Mar2026 CWIP₹5.4314cr [S01]|Commissioning dates, saleable output and incremental margins|Further capex before prior capacity's economics are disclosed|Repeated delay/cost growth plus unfunded capex or persistent poor mature returns.|\\n|FY26 exports₹3.8904cr [S01]|Geography/customer mix, export collections and contribution|Further decline without profitable cash-generating domestic replacement|Loss of key customers undermines the base demand/margin path; reassessDUR/EXEC rather than using country-count claims.|\\n|31Mar2026 accessible cash subtotal₹14.4435cr; debt₹0.2038cr [S01]|Actual interim cash plus committed facility and covenant evidence|Cash falls below modeled₹1cr buffer at any payment date|Verified unfinanceable essential obligation is G3Fail; an uncommitted issue is not a cure.|\\n|FY21–23 director-loan violations, FY25 direction discrepancy [S02/S05–07]|Signed RPT direction, repayment and statutory resolution|Fresh unexplained related advances or unresolved contradictory disclosure|Substantiated severe diversion/repeated minority harm triggersG1Fail/GOVoverride, not merely an auditor change.|\\n|19Oct market window39/60observed, lot1200 [market extract]|Complete zero-trade/status evidence and contemporaneous permitted-order depth|Daily5% participation remains below one whole lot or missing sessions persist|No reliable executable exit for the intended size: G4Hold/new-purchaseIASNS remains.|\\n\\nUpdate log: 20 October 2026 cutoff, first v3.0 research edition. Primary original-audit findings and official revised FY26 report supersede an initially discovered mirror; no earlier point score is silently revised. The official 104-page annual report matches the revised AGM attachment after its cover; the older mirror omitted an EPS/dividend accounting-policy paragraph but did not change the financial figures. Any future score change must distinguish new economics, evidence resolution and price effects.\\n\\n\"","\"Classification and final quality checks\"","\"\\n**Classification: Hold — unresolved financial reliability and tradability. Pending evidence; excluded from investable ranking; no compounder or attractive-entry label.** Do not rank NS below scored companies as if it were a proven low-quality result. Do not compare this score arithmetically with older framework versions. No overall SME winner or portfolio exit count follows from a single-company report.\\n\\nThe following 16 checks are delivery checks, not claims that every missing item has been resolved:\\n\\n| # | Required check | Result |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Correct issuer/security/scope and explicit dates | Verified identity; standalone; stale quote explicitly dated. |\\n| 2 | Primary records; management/analyst attribution | Primary audit/results/prospectus/peer/market sources; forecast assumptions labelled. |\\n| 3 | Audit/RPT/funding/dilution and proportionate gates | Original adverse history retained; no unsupported fraud allegation; G2/G4 holds. |\\n| 4 | Comparable units/periods; no false TTM or quarterly CFO | ₹crore; restatement bridges; H1 not added to FY; FY27 cash ND. |\\n| 5 | Revenue versus profits/acquisitions/pass-through/base | Real-volume bridge ND and REV NS; Covid anchor and value-added cross-check explicit. |\\n| 6 | Ratio-of-sums CFO/PAT; capex distinction | Exact 3/5-year sums; separate CFO versus FCF and provision sensitivity. |\\n| 7 | Incremental return numerator/denominator/timing | Broad 2/3/4-year proxy with financing/asset residuals; five-year original-basis diagnostic separately flagged as non-harmonized. |\\n| 8 | Normalization, tax, claims, leases, dilution | Other-income/exception treatment; booked/unknown claims separated; no disclosed material lease liability assumed zero without noting source scope. |\\n| 9 | Linked scenarios and funding, no double counting | Pre-entry/monthly/intraperiod/terminal cash; whole-share limits; immutable failure; one debt deduction. |\\n| 10 | Canonical factors and correct weights | 13 factors, 4 gates, each score weights100%; Q uses unrounded final BQS. |\\n| 11 | Missingness/bounds/confidence | NS retained, no reweight, exact bounds; Low overall material confidence. |\\n| 12 | Raw/final/caps | All computable pieces shown; no unsupported cap; G2/G4 force no favorable IAS. |\\n| 13 | Matrix and compounder rules | Incomplete/hold classification, no invented quality band. |\\n| 14 | Stated position liquidity and stale quote | ₹5lakh,1200-share lot,2/5/10% participation diagnostics; actual execution NS. |\\n| 15 | Strongest bear and thesis-break | Collectibility/provision settlement/capital absorption explicitly linked. |\\n| 16 | Common methodology and incomplete status | v3.0 provenance retained; no ranking or batch-completion claim. |\\n\\n**Bottom line:** Royal Arc has an established operating business. What is not established is that recent revenue growth translates into collectible, capital-efficient per-share value at an executable price. Resolving that distinction is more important than squeezing a point target from uncertain accounts.\\n\\n\"","\"Sources, evidence and reproduction\"","\"\\n- **S01** [FY26 audited annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Annual_Report_RAEL_FY_2025-2026.pdf), 2026-08, pages/sections 66-104.\\n- **S02** [FY25 corrected annual report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ROYALARC_09092025161845_CORRIGENDUM_TO_THE_ANNUAL_REPORT_FOR_FY_2024-2025.pdf), 2025-09-09, pages/sections 65-102.\\n- **S03** [Final IPO prospectus](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Propsectus-Royal-Arc-18.02.2025.pdf), 2025-02-18, pages/sections 32-60,143-162,201-239.\\n- **S04** [FY24 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Annual-Report-2023-24.pdf), 2024-07-03 audit, pages/sections 37-72.\\n- **S05** [FY23 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2022-23.pdf), 2023 audit, pages/sections 2-35.\\n- **S06** [FY22 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2021-22.pdf), 2022 audit, pages/sections 2-36.\\n- **S07** [FY21 original annual report](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2020-21.pdf), 2021 audit, pages/sections 2-33.\\n- **S08** [H1 FY26 results](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Outcome-of-BM-10-Nov-2025.pdf), 2025-11-10, pages/sections financial statements.\\n- **S09** [FY26 audited results](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ROYALARC_19052026171747_Outcome_of_BM_19052026.pdf), 2026-05-19, pages/sections all20pages.\\n- **S10** [GEE FY26 annual report](https://www.geelimited.com/uploads/gee_reports/6c455225b15b0f70b908be16a04c7d6e.pdf), 2026, pages/sections 76-80.\\n- **S11** [ESAB FY26 annual report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/chaithanya_lakshmi_esab_co_in_03072026114500_Notice_of_Shareholders_Meeting_2026_Signed.pdf), 2026-07-03, pages/sections 106-108.\\n- **S12** [FY26 shareholding](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/SHP-31.03.2026-RAEL.html), 2026-03-31, pages/sections alltables.\\n- **S13** [FY19 original annual accounts](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2018-19.pdf), 2019 original audited year, pages/sections FY19 audit pp5-13; FY20 audit pp2-11 and financials pp12-14, note8p21.\\n- **S14** [FY20 original annual accounts](https://royalarc.in/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Annual_Report_2019-20.pdf), 2020 original audited year, pages/sections FY19 audit pp5-13; FY20 audit pp2-11 and financials pp12-14, note8p21.\\n- **S15** [Ador Welding FY26 revised audited standalone results](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/ADORWELD_29042026203614_revisedAFR.pdf), 2026-04-29, pages/sections PDF pp5-10; P&L p5; segments p6; cashflow p8; normalization notes9-10.\\n- **S16** [ESAB July7 corrigendum and revised FY26 annual report](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/corporate/chaithanya_lakshmi_esab_co_in_07072026121432_Correigendum_signed.pdf), 2026-07-07, pages/sections Cover and printed107-108 (PDF109-110).\\n\\nMarket source family: [NSE 19 October 2026 security master](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/interop/NSE_CM_security_19102026.csv.gz) and [19 October bhavcopy](https://nsearchives.nseindia.com/content/cm/BhavCopy_NSE_CM_0_0_0_20261019_F_0000.csv.zip). The authored market extract retains all 60 exact daily URLs, ZIP/CSV hashes and matching rows; absent rows remain explicitly absent. Latest actual Royal observation is 16 October, not the master file's date. These are official sources, not evidence of currently available bid depth.\\n\\nSource register gives document identifiers, public URLs, dates, page locators and hashes. Evidence ledger distinguishes V verified disclosure, C calculation, M management statement and A analyst assumption; contradictions and confidence are explicit. Reproduction requires only the declared compact JSON/Markdown inputs and standard-library Python code. The full archive additionally contains generated canonical outputs, complete compressed path events and audit tables. Neither package includes raw PDFs, OCR, screenshots, download helpers, private identifiers, authentication material or external file-service identities.\\n\\nRun `python build.py`, then `python verify.py`. In a freshly extracted recovery copy run `python guarded_rebuild.py --clean`: generated outputs and old attestations are removed, undeclared data reads/network/subprocess calls are blocked during rebuilding, then hashes and arithmetic are independently checked. Packaging/recovery attestations describe actual tests; their presence alone is not evidence that a reconstruction succeeded. No external account action or trade is implied by these research artifacts.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"original_heading\"","78","2405","\"## 1. Executive verdict\\n\"","2444","5382","\"## 2. Research header, scope and gates\\n\"","5408","12450","\"## 3. Canonical scorecard\\n\"","12492","14990","\"## 4. Business model and economic drivers\\n\"","15026","16752","\"## 5. Competitive position and moat\\n\"","16794","22832","\"## 6. Revenue growth and earnings quality\\n\"","22879","26311","\"## 7. Growth runway and reinvestment economics\\n\"","26353","32456","\"## 8. Governance, ownership and execution\\n\"","32500","37086","\"## 9. Financial forensics, cash and funding\\n\"","37151","49706","\"## 10. Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\\n\"","49750","51216","\"## 11. Strongest bear case and failure path\\n\"","51255","52467","\"## 12. Multibagger and compounder test\\n\"","52520","57888","\"## 13. Monitoring plan and explicit resolution tests\\n\"","57935","60959","\"## 14. Classification and final quality checks\\n\"","61001","65588","\"## 15. Sources, evidence and reproduction\\n\"","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"Complete JSON content\"","\"25deeb9563b97dc7bf376b7bea028dc46fad0eb4d5a2a51f57fba43a0a19e4b1\"","\"Authored analytical table or source register, not a bundled original filing. Source-access qualificatio